export const EXTRA_UNITS = [{"id":"u4","title":"Complete Deficiency Standards","chapters":["ch9","ch10","ch11"]},{"id":"u5","title":"Building Systems & Certificates","chapters":["ch12","ch13"]}];
export const EXTRA_CHAPTERS = [{"id":"ch9","unit":"u4","number":9,"title":"Unit Deficiencies — Complete Standards","subtitle":"The working reference for the Unit inspectable area — criteria, measurements, exclusions, and worked calls","sections":[{"heading":"How to use this chapter","body":"This is reference material, not narrative. Read it once end to end so you know what is in it, then use it as a lookup while you build your own field reference from Chapter 3.\n\nEach family below gives you:\n• The STANDARD, named as HUD publishes it where the name is settled.\n• The CRITERIA that decide whether a condition is a deficiency, with the measurable number wherever one exists.\n• The EXCLUSIONS — conditions that look citable but are not.\n• WORKED CALLS — real observations with the correct answer and the reasoning, including the borderline ones.\n\nTHREE RULES BEFORE YOU START\n\n1. VERIFY AGAINST THE CURRENT PUBLISHED STANDARD. HUD versions the standards and revises them. Everything here reflects the criteria as published through mid-2026 and should be checked against the current version before you put it in a client deliverable. Write the version date on your own reference.\n\n2. MEASURE, DO NOT ESTIMATE. The recurring numbers in this chapter are: 1/2 inch (exposed conductor opening), 3/4 inch (trip hazard vertical, elevator mis-level), 2 inches (trip hazard horizontal), 2 to 6 inches (water heater TPR discharge termination), 3 feet (combustibles from a heat source), 6 feet (GFCI from a water source), 18 inches (sprinkler obstruction), 4 or more risers (handrail required), 30 inches (guardrail required above grade, and minimum guardrail height), 4 square inches / 1 square foot / 9 square feet (mold escalation), 68 degrees Fahrenheit (HVAC), 5 to 9 millimeters (cockroach egg case). Carry a tape measure and a ruler. Photograph the measurement.\n\n3. THE SIX DEFERRED-SCORING CATEGORIES. Fire-labeled doors, GFCI/AFCI, guardrails, HVAC, interior lighting, and minimum electrical and lighting are cited and must be corrected, but do not deduct points until October 1, 2026. Mark them in your notes so your projection and your punch list stay separable — the client needs both numbers.\n\nA note on severity in this chapter. Where a severity tier is stated, it reflects the criteria as published. Where a standard escalates by measurement or extent, the escalation is described. Where you cannot confirm a tier for a specific program, record the standard name, the location and the measurement, and resolve the tier against the current standard rather than guessing — a report that says \"confirm tier against current standard\" is professional; a report that guesses is a liability."},{"heading":"Electrical — conductors, outlets, switches and GFCI","body":"STANDARD: ELECTRICAL — CONDUCTOR, OUTLET, AND SWITCH\nThe deficiency is any exposed electrical conductor that presents a hazard — a conductor that is not enclosed or properly insulated.\n\nHUD's listed examples:\n• Damaged sheathing on a conductor.\n• An open port.\n• A missing knockout.\n• A missing outlet or switch cover.\n• A missing breaker or fuse.\n• A missing lightbulb leaving an open socket.\n• An opening or gap greater than 1/2 INCH.\n\nThe 1/2-inch figure is the criterion that settles most disputes. Measure it.\n\nRelated conditions you will encounter in the same walk: outlets or switches that do not function, damaged or broken devices, scorching or heat damage around a device, and loose devices that move in the box.\n\nSTANDARD: ELECTRICAL — GFCI OR AFCI OUTLET OR BREAKER\nThe affirmative requirement is that OUTLETS WITHIN SIX FEET OF A WATER SOURCE MUST BE GFCI PROTECTED. Protection may be provided at the receptacle or at the breaker.\nA GFCI outlet or breaker whose test or reset button is MISSING in a way that results in an exposed conductor is evaluated under the Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch standard instead.\nInterim repair: a missing GFCI receptacle may be interim-repaired with a blank cover plate, but the full repair must follow within a reasonable timeframe approved by HUD or its designee.\nSCORING NOTE: GFCI/AFCI is one of the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — measure the opening\nA bedroom outlet has a cover plate with one corner broken off, leaving a gap you measure at 3/8 inch. No conductor is visible.\nAnswer: under the 1/2-inch criterion and no exposed conductor. Not a deficiency. Recommend replacement — plates cost pennies and a 3/8-inch break becomes a 3/4-inch break — but do not score it.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the missing knockout\nA surface-mounted junction box above the kitchen counter has a missing knockout leaving a 7/8-inch round opening with conductors visible inside.\nAnswer: exposed conductor. Over the 1/2-inch criterion, and HUD lists a missing knockout explicitly. Correction is a knockout seal, under a dollar.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the open socket\nA bedroom ceiling fixture is missing its bulb; the socket is open and the fixture is energized.\nAnswer: HUD lists a missing lightbulb among the examples of an exposed conductor. Cite it. This one surprises people constantly and it is free to fix — carry bulbs.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — GFCI distance\nA kitchen has a receptacle 3 feet from the sink with no GFCI protection at the device, and a second receptacle across the room, 9 feet from the sink, also unprotected.\nAnswer: the 3-foot receptacle is within 6 feet of a water source and the affirmative requirement is unmet. The 9-foot receptacle is outside the criterion. Check the panel before concluding — protection may be provided at the breaker, in which case both are protected and neither is cited.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the bathroom with a protected circuit\nA bathroom receptacle is a standard receptacle with no test or reset button, but the panel shows a GFCI breaker serving the bathroom circuit and the receptacle trips when tested.\nAnswer: not a deficiency. Protection at the breaker satisfies the requirement. This is the most common false positive in the entire program — always test rather than assuming from the device's appearance.\n\nWORKED CALL 6 — scorching\nAn outlet in a living room shows brown heat discoloration radiating from one slot and the plastic is deformed.\nAnswer: a damaged device with evidence of a heat event. Cite it, and treat it as urgent regardless of how you ultimately classify it. Recommend the circuit be evaluated, not just the device replaced."},{"heading":"Electrical — panels, lighting and the minimum requirements","body":"PANELS AND OVERCURRENT PROTECTION IN THE UNIT\nWhere a panel or subpanel is inside the dwelling:\n• A missing breaker or fuse leaving an open port is an exposed-conductor deficiency.\n• A missing or damaged panel cover or deadfront is an exposed-conductor deficiency.\n• Panels must be accessible — blocked access is a deficiency in its own right and also prevents the resident from restoring power safely.\n• Look for double-tapped breakers, unsupported or improperly terminated conductors, and evidence of heat.\n\nSTANDARD: MINIMUM ELECTRICAL AND LIGHTING (affirmative requirement)\n• EVERY HABITABLE ROOM must have TWO WORKING OUTLETS, or ONE WORKING OUTLET AND A PERMANENTLY INSTALLED LIGHT FIXTURE.\n• There must be AT LEAST ONE PERMANENTLY INSTALLED LIGHT FIXTURE in the KITCHEN and in the BATHROOM.\nSCORING NOTE: minimum electrical and lighting, and interior lighting, are two of the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026.\n\nSTANDARD: INTERIOR LIGHTING (affirmative requirement)\nPermanently installed interior lighting must be present where required and must function. A fixture that is present but inoperable does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nWHAT COUNTS AS A HABITABLE ROOM. Living rooms, dining rooms and bedrooms are habitable. Bathrooms, hallways, closets, utility spaces and storage rooms generally are not. Kitchens carry their own explicit fixture requirement. When a floor plan has an ambiguous space — a den, a bonus room, a converted porch — describe it precisely in your notes rather than deciding silently, because the answer changes the count.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — counting the bedroom\nA bedroom has one working outlet, one dead outlet, and a ceiling fixture that works.\nAnswer: one WORKING outlet plus a permanently installed light fixture satisfies the requirement. Compliant on the minimum standard. The dead outlet is separately a non-functioning device and should be recorded on its own.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the lamp defense\nA bedroom has one working outlet and no ceiling fixture. The resident has a floor lamp plugged in.\nAnswer: not compliant. The requirement is a PERMANENTLY INSTALLED fixture. A lamp is furniture. Expect to have this conversation with a manager, and expect to be right.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the kitchen with under-cabinet lighting only\nA kitchen has plug-in under-cabinet strip lights and no ceiling fixture.\nAnswer: not compliant. The kitchen requires at least one permanently installed light fixture, and a plug-in strip is not permanently installed.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the fixture that does not work\nA bathroom has a permanently installed vanity fixture with no bulb and a burned-out ballast.\nAnswer: two things at once. Present-but-inoperable does not satisfy the interior lighting requirement, and the open socket from the missing bulb is an exposed-conductor condition under the Electrical standard. Record both, under both standard names.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the whole-property pattern\nAcross a 96-unit property built to one floor plan, every bedroom has a single outlet and no ceiling fixture.\nAnswer: the minimum electrical and lighting requirement is unmet property-wide. Within the Unit area the duplicate rule collapses this to one scored instance — but the correction is 96 units of electrical work, and the scoring begins October 1, 2026. This is the exact case where you tell the client the score exposure is modest and the capital exposure is large, and where planning the scope over eighteen months beats discovering it inside a 30-day correction window."},{"heading":"Plumbing — supply, drainage, leaks and the water heater","body":"STANDARD: LEAK (PLUMBING)\nA plumbing leak is a failure of a plumbing system that allows water intrusion in unintended areas.\nWhat inspectors examine: all visible plumbing connections, including traps, supply lines, and direct connections to fixtures.\nWhat counts as evidence even without an active drip: mold or mildew, peeling paint or wallpaper, warped or stained walls, and buckled or water-stained floors or ceilings.\n\nAFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENT: HOT AND COLD WATER MUST RUN FROM SINKS.\n\nSTANDARD: WATER HEATER\nDeficiencies include:\n• The temperature and pressure relief (TPR) valve has an active leak, or is obstructed such that it cannot be fully actuated.\n• Relief valve discharge piping that is damaged, capped, sloped upward, or made of unsuitable material.\n• Discharge piping that terminates MORE THAN 6 INCHES or LESS THAN 2 INCHES above the flood level of the waste receptor — a drain pan or floor drain. The compliant window is 2 TO 6 INCHES.\nWater heater TPR deficiencies are treated as Severe health and safety findings.\nAlso examine: the flue or vent connection (see the gas and chimney criteria in Chapter 5), combustible storage within three feet, and whether the appliance is properly supported and accessible.\n\nDRAINAGE AND FIXTURES\nFixtures that will not drain, drain slowly enough to back up, or discharge to an improper location. Missing or damaged traps. Sewage or wastewater present where it should not be — treat wastewater intrusion as an urgent condition and escalate it immediately rather than logging it for the report.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the three water heaters\nUnit A: TPR discharge terminates 4 inches above the floor drain. Unit B: 11 inches. Unit C: 1 inch, resting inside the drain pan.\nAnswer: A compliant. B deficient — above the 6-inch limit. C deficient — below the 2-inch limit and at risk of blockage. Three units, one tape measure, two findings.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the capped pipe\nA discharge pipe has a threaded cap on the end, installed by maintenance to stop a drip.\nAnswer: capped discharge piping is a listed deficiency. Explain it in plain terms to the client: the cap turns a relief valve into a pressure vessel. And note the underlying drip is itself a TPR valve leak, which is a second listed condition.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the leak with no drip\nA kitchen sink cabinet floor is swollen and delaminating with staining at the base, but nothing is dripping at the moment of inspection.\nAnswer: the evidence supports a plumbing leak finding. Check for mold-like substance in the same cabinet — if present, that is a SECOND finding under a separate standard, measured against its own 4-square-inch, 1-square-foot and 9-square-foot thresholds.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the upward slope\nA discharge pipe leaves the valve, drops, then rises about 8 inches before terminating over the pan.\nAnswer: upward-sloping discharge piping is a listed deficiency. The rise traps water and defeats the discharge path.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — hot water\nA unit's kitchen sink produces hot water after about ninety seconds; the bathroom sink produces only cold no matter how long it runs.\nAnswer: the bathroom sink fails the affirmative requirement that hot and cold water run from sinks. The kitchen delay is a distribution characteristic, not a deficiency. Test every fixture and give each one time."},{"heading":"Kitchen — appliances and components","body":"AFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENT: A REFRIGERATOR MUST BE PRESENT.\nAFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENT: AT LEAST ONE PERMANENTLY INSTALLED LIGHT FIXTURE IN THE KITCHEN.\nAFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENT: HOT AND COLD RUNNING WATER FROM THE SINK.\n\nBeyond presence, kitchen components that exist must function and must not present a hazard.\n\nWHAT TO INSPECT, IN ORDER\n• Refrigerator — present, running, cooling, door seals intact, no damage that prevents it closing.\n• Cooking appliance — every burner ignites and holds flame or heats; the oven heats; controls function; the appliance is stable and does not tip; the anti-tip device is present where the appliance requires one.\n• Sink — hot and cold water, drains properly, no leaks at supply or trap, no damage to the basin creating a hazard.\n• Countertops and cabinets — damage creating a hazard, particularly delaminated edges and exposed fasteners; doors and drawers that function.\n• Ventilation over the cooking appliance where installed — present and operating.\n• Electrical — GFCI protection within 6 feet of the sink, cover plates, and outlets that function.\n• Storage of combustibles near the range.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the partial range\nA four-burner electric range has one element that does not heat. The oven works.\nAnswer: the appliance is present but partially inoperable. Cite it. Typically an inexpensive element or a service call, and the ratio of points recovered to dollars spent is good.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the resident's refrigerator\nA unit's refrigerator was removed by management and the resident brought their own, which works.\nAnswer: the affirmative requirement is that a refrigerator be present. Record the situation precisely — including whose appliance it is and whether it functions — and resolve the ownership question with the client, because a resident-owned appliance leaves the unit non-compliant the day the resident moves out with it.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the anti-tip bracket\nA freestanding range rocks forward when the open oven door is pressed; no anti-tip device is installed.\nAnswer: an unstable cooking appliance is a genuine hazard, particularly with children in the household. Record it with the specific observation — \"range tips forward under load on open door, no anti-tip device installed\" — rather than a general note about the stove.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the delaminated counter\nA countertop edge near the sink has delaminated, leaving a lifted laminate edge with exposed particle board and a raised metal edge strip.\nAnswer: check the sharp-edge criterion — a sharp edge that can result in a cut or puncture hazard LIKELY TO REQUIRE EMERGENCY CARE such as stitches is the standard's test. A lifted laminate edge that could cut a hand but would not plausibly require stitches is not a sharp-edge citation; a protruding metal strip with a cutting edge may be. Describe what you see precisely and let the criterion decide.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — combustibles by the range\nA can of cooking spray and a bottle of charcoal lighter fluid sit on the counter beside the range, roughly 10 inches from a burner.\nAnswer: combustibles stored within three feet of a heat appliance is a recorded deficiency. Zero-cost correction. Include it in the resident notice, since it recurs constantly and is entirely preventable."},{"heading":"Bathroom — fixtures and ventilation","body":"AFFIRMATIVE REQUIREMENT: AT LEAST ONE PERMANENTLY INSTALLED LIGHT FIXTURE IN THE BATHROOM.\n\nSTANDARD: VENTILATION\nNSPIRE recognizes THREE acceptable forms of bathroom ventilation:\n1. A mechanical exhaust fan.\n2. A passive ventilation system.\n3. An operable window — one that opens to allow air and moisture to escape, opens easily, and is not sealed shut or painted over.\nWhere NONE of the three is present and operable, the bathroom is a MODERATE deficiency.\n\nWHAT ELSE TO INSPECT\n• Toilet — secure to the floor, no rocking, flushes and refills, no leak at the base or supply, no cracks in the bowl or tank.\n• Tub or shower — drains, no active leak, surround intact, no damage creating a hazard, and no mold-like substance beyond the de minimis floor.\n• Sink — hot and cold water, drains, no leak at supply or trap.\n• GFCI protection within 6 feet of the water source.\n• Mold-like substance — measure cumulatively per room against the 4-square-inch, 1-square-foot and 9-square-foot thresholds.\n• Ceiling and wall surfaces for water staining, which points to a leak above or a ventilation failure.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the three bathrooms\nA: no fan, no passive vent, window painted shut. B: no fan, no passive vent, window opens freely. C: fan present and audibly running but moving no air, no window.\nAnswer: A is a Moderate deficiency — a painted-shut window is not operable. B is compliant. C is a Moderate deficiency — present but not operable does not satisfy the standard. Use the tissue test on C and photograph the tissue not sticking.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the fan that vents into the attic\nA bathroom fan runs and moves air, but the duct terminates in the attic rather than to the exterior.\nAnswer: describe the condition precisely and evaluate it against the ventilation criterion — the purpose is to allow air and moisture to ESCAPE. Discharging into an enclosed attic drives moisture into the structure and reliably produces mold-like substance findings later. Even where you cannot conclusively place it as a ventilation citation, flag it as a root cause with a written recommendation; that is exactly the kind of finding a client cannot get from their maintenance staff.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — measuring the mold\nA bathroom shows discoloration around the fan grille of roughly 18 by 24 inches, plus two patches behind the toilet of about 6 by 6 inches each.\nAnswer: 432 plus 72 square inches equals 504 square inches, which is 3.5 square feet cumulative in the room. That lands in the 1-to-9-square-foot band — Severe. Measure and add; do not eyeball. Then look for the moisture source, which in this case is almost certainly the fan.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the rocking toilet\nA toilet moves about half an inch when pressed sideways, and there is a faint stain on the floor at the base.\nAnswer: two related conditions. The loose fixture is a deficiency in its own right, and the staining supports a leak finding at the wax seal. Both should be recorded, because a client who tightens the bolts without replacing the seal will have the same finding next year.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the missing bathroom light\nA bathroom has no permanently installed fixture; light comes from a plug-in vanity lamp on the counter.\nAnswer: the affirmative requirement is unmet. Note that interior lighting and minimum electrical and lighting sit in the six deferred-scoring categories — cited and corrected now, deducting from October 1, 2026."},{"heading":"Structural — walls, floors, ceilings, stairs and handrails","body":"WALLS, CEILINGS AND FLOORS\nWhat makes a surface condition a deficiency is a HAZARD or a HABITABILITY consequence, not appearance. Look for:\n• Holes through a surface, particularly those exposing framing, insulation, wiring or an adjacent space.\n• Water staining, sagging or bulging ceilings, which indicate an active or recent leak above.\n• Floor covering damage creating a trip or laceration hazard — separated carpet seams with a raised edge, curled vinyl, broken tile with sharp fragments.\n• Substrate damage — soft, spongy or deflecting floors indicating water damage or structural failure.\n• Deteriorated painted surfaces, which in pre-1978 housing carry lead-safe-work-practice implications that sit alongside NSPIRE and should be flagged to the client.\n\nINTERIOR STAIRS AND HANDRAILS\n• A HANDRAIL IS REQUIRED WHERE THERE ARE FOUR OR MORE RISERS. A stair with four or more risers and no handrail is a deficiency.\n• Handrails that are present must be secure. A handrail that moves under load does not serve its purpose.\n• Treads and risers must be sound — no broken, missing, loose or deflecting treads.\n\nGUARDRAILS\n• A GUARDRAIL IS REQUIRED WHERE AN ELEVATED WALKING SURFACE IS 30 INCHES OR MORE ABOVE GRADE.\n• Where required, the guardrail must be AT LEAST 30 INCHES IN HEIGHT.\n• Missing, loose, damaged, and undersized guardrails are all deficiencies.\nSCORING NOTE: guardrail is one of the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026 — cited, corrected, not deducting until that date.\n\nTRIP HAZARDS INSIDE THE UNIT\nThe measurable criteria: a 3/4-INCH difference in level in the walking path for a vertical hazard; at least 2 INCHES of separation for a horizontal hazard.\n\nSHARP EDGES\nThe test is whether a sharp edge on or adjacent to the built environment can result in a cut or puncture hazard LIKELY TO REQUIRE EMERGENCY CARE, for example stitches.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — counting risers\nAn interior stair from the entry to the living level has four risers and no handrail on either side.\nAnswer: four or more risers requires a handrail. Deficiency. Count risers, do not estimate by eye — three versus four is the whole call.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the handrail that moves\nA stair handrail is present but the bottom bracket has pulled from the wall and the rail deflects about 2 inches under hand pressure.\nAnswer: a handrail that is not secure does not serve its purpose. Deficiency. Record the deflection you observed.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — measuring the guardrail\nA split-level unit has an interior landing 34 inches above the floor below, with a guardrail measuring 27 inches high.\nAnswer: at 34 inches above the lower surface a guardrail is required, and the installed rail is undersized against the 30-inch minimum. Deficiency, in the deferred-scoring group.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — carpet versus tile\nA bedroom carpet has a separated seam with a raised edge across the doorway. A hallway is missing one vinyl tile, leaving a flush recess in the subfloor.\nAnswer: the carpet seam creates a hazard and is a deficiency. The flush tile recess creates no vertical differential and is cosmetic. If the recess were deep enough to produce a 3/4-inch differential in the walking path, the trip hazard standard would apply — so measure before deciding.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the ceiling stain\nA bedroom ceiling has a 3-foot brown stain with slight sagging at the center. No visible mold-like substance, nothing dripping.\nAnswer: the staining and sag indicate a leak above. Record the surface condition and the evidence of water intrusion, look immediately for the source — a unit above, a roof, a supply line — and check the same ceiling and walls for mold-like substance measured against the thresholds. A stain with no visible growth is not a mold citation; it is a warning that one is coming."},{"heading":"Doors, windows, egress and safety devices","body":"EGRESS — ALWAYS TOP TIER\n• All egress doors must open and close completely and be free of obstructions.\n• Double-key cylinder deadbolts, and any lock requiring a key, tool, or special knowledge or effort from the EGRESS SIDE, are prohibited on any door serving as an exit or along the exit access.\n• Any window usable as a means of egress, primary or otherwise, must open FULLY. Window locks requiring a key, tool or special knowledge from the egress side are cited as blocked egress.\n• A PERMANENTLY INSTALLED window-mounted air conditioner in an egress window is cited as blocked egress.\n• Obstructions count even when resident-owned. Furniture blocking a bedroom door or an exterior door is a deficiency.\n\nUNIT ENTRY DOORS\nThe severity of unit entry door deficiencies was raised to LIFE-THREATENING, even where the door is not a fire-labeled door. A missing entry door, or one that will not close or latch, is a top-tier finding.\n\nDOOR SECURITY (Severe under the security language)\nA door that closes and latches but cannot be LOCKED — broken deadbolt, missing lockset, split strike plate that fails under pressure — seriously compromises the resident's physical security.\n\nWINDOWS\n• Windows must operate as designed. Painted-shut, broken-sash and missing-hardware conditions are deficiencies.\n• Broken or missing glazing.\n• A window that cannot be secured, particularly at grade level, sits in the security family.\n\nFIRE-LABELED DOORS\nFire-labeled doors must be intact, self-closing and self-latching where required, with the label present and legible and no field modifications defeating the rating.\nSCORING NOTE: fire-labeled doors are one of the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements.\n\nSMOKE AND CARBON MONOXIDE ALARMS\n• Smoke alarms per NFPA 72 or successor: on each level and inside each sleeping area, plus outside sleeping areas.\n• Alarms must be HARDWIRED or have a SEALED, TAMPER-RESISTANT 10-YEAR BATTERY. For units built or substantially rehabilitated after DECEMBER 2022, hardwired is the only option.\n• Carbon monoxide detection per chapters 9 and 11 of the 2018 International Fire Code, required in both the Inside area and the Unit.\n• These requirements are statutory and were not part of any NSPIRE compliance extension.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the security screen\nA ground-floor bedroom window has a security grille installed on the exterior with no interior release mechanism.\nAnswer: the window cannot function as a means of egress without a tool or special knowledge. Blocked egress. Grilles with a compliant interior quick-release are a different matter — test the release rather than assuming from the appearance.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the door that will not latch versus the door that will not lock\nUnit 118: warped frame, the door closes but the latch does not engage and the door drifts open. Unit 210: the door closes and latches, but the deadbolt spins freely.\nAnswer: 118 is a unit entry door finding at the top tier — the door cannot perform its function. 210 is Severe under the security language. Different tiers, different weights, and the distinction is worth getting right.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — counting alarms\nA three-bedroom, two-story unit has one alarm in the upstairs hall and one in the downstairs living room. No alarms inside any bedroom.\nAnswer: coverage is deficient. NFPA 72 placement requires alarms inside each sleeping area as well as outside them. Count sleeping rooms and levels on every walk — this is arithmetic, not judgment.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the compliant-looking alarm\nA 2019-constructed unit has a sealed 10-year battery alarm, correctly placed and working.\nAnswer: for construction or substantial rehabilitation after December 2022, hardwired is the only option — but this unit was built in 2019, so the sealed 10-year battery satisfies the requirement. Get the construction or rehab date before you make this call; it changes the answer.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the seasonal sweep\nIn July, six of twelve sampled units have window air conditioners. Four are permanently installed in bedroom egress windows; two are portable units hosed through windows that still open fully.\nAnswer: four blocked-egress findings, two compliant installations. Within the Unit area the duplicate rule collapses the four to one scored instance — but all four must be corrected, each on a 24-hour clock. This is why the seasonal resident notice belongs in your standard client package."},{"heading":"HVAC, environmental conditions and the unit walk order","body":"STANDARD: HVAC\n• When the OUTSIDE temperature is BELOW 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT, the resident must be able to maintain a MINIMUM OF 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT inside through a safe heating source.\n• When the outside temperature is 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT OR ABOVE, the air conditioning system or device must be OPERATIONAL.\n• A permanently installed heating source is required for climate zones HUD designates by notice.\n• UNVENTED SPACE HEATERS burning gas, oil or kerosene are PROHIBITED in units.\nSCORING NOTE: HVAC is one of the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements — cited and required to be corrected, not deducting until October 1, 2026.\n\nMOLD-LIKE SUBSTANCE\nCumulative per room: under 4 square inches not recorded; more than 4 square inches to under 1 square foot Moderate; 1 to 9 square feet Severe; more than 9 square feet Life-Threatening. Visual identification — patches or spots, white, green, yellow, gray, brown or black, often fuzzy or cottony. Odor is an indicator, not diagnostic; visual confirmation is required.\n\nINFESTATION\nEvidence is sufficient without live pests: droppings, insect eggs, shed skins, dead insects, strong odors. Cockroach egg cases are brown oblong cases roughly 5 to 9 millimeters long. Within a unit, more than three live roaches simultaneously in daylight, or at least one in more than one location, indicates extensive infestation. Across multiple units and common areas, the condition escalates to the top tier on a 24-hour clock.\n\nFLAMMABLE AND COMBUSTIBLE ITEMS\nCombustibles — hairspray, nail polish remover, butane or charcoal lighter fluid, paint thinner in original containers — must not be stored WITHIN THREE FEET of a heat appliance or fuel-burning water heater. Storage on or near ignition sources and improperly stored chemicals are likewise deficiencies.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — testing heat in the wrong season\nYou walk a property in August; the outside temperature is 84 degrees.\nAnswer: the HVAC criterion is seasonal. At 68 degrees or above outside, what must be operational is the air conditioning system or device. You cannot meaningfully test the heating performance criterion on that day. Record that heating was not testable, inspect the equipment for evident defects and safety conditions, and schedule a heating check before the property's cold-season window. Saying \"not testable on this date, scheduled for October\" is professional; guessing is not.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the space heater\nA unit has a kerosene space heater in the living room, in use, with the flue-less design typical of portable units.\nAnswer: unvented space heaters burning gas, oil or kerosene are prohibited in units. Remove it, and investigate WHY it is there — a resident using a kerosene heater usually has an underlying heating failure that is its own finding.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the AC that does not cool\nOutside temperature 79 degrees. A unit's central AC runs, the fan moves air, but the supply air is the same temperature as the return.\nAnswer: at 68 degrees or above outside the system must be OPERATIONAL. A system that runs but does not cool is not operational in any useful sense. Record the supply and return temperatures — a number turns an argument into evidence.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the combined bathroom finding\nA bathroom has 2 square feet of mold-like substance, a fan that runs but moves no air, and staining on the ceiling around a supply line penetration.\nAnswer: potentially three findings under three standards — mold at Severe in the 1-to-9-square-foot band, ventilation as a Moderate, and a plumbing leak supported by the staining. Record all three by standard name, then tell the client the sequence: fix the leak, fix the fan, then remediate the mold. Remediating first guarantees a repeat finding.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the evidence-only infestation\nA vacant unit shows mouse droppings along the baseboard behind the range and gnaw marks on the cabinet toe-kick. No live rodents.\nAnswer: citable on evidence alone. Advise closing entry points, not just cleaning — a client who only cleans will be cited again next year, and the service records from a pest management contract are also what support a beyond-owner-control argument in a technical review.\n\nTHE UNIT WALK ORDER\nDo it the same way every time and you stop missing things:\n1. Entry door — closes, latches, locks, and the frame and strike are sound.\n2. Every window — operate each one, check the lock, look for a permanently installed AC in an egress window.\n3. Every smoke and CO alarm — presence, placement by sleeping area and level, power source, and function.\n4. Electrical panel if accessible — cover, open ports, breakers.\n5. Kitchen — refrigerator, every burner, oven, sink hot and cold, GFCI, cover plates, combustibles near the range.\n6. Bathroom — ventilation by tissue test, GFCI, fixtures, and measure any mold-like substance.\n7. Water heater — TPR valve, and MEASURE the discharge pipe termination.\n8. Each habitable room — count working outlets and permanent fixtures, check cover plates, look at floors, walls and ceilings.\n9. HVAC — season-appropriate test, and look for unvented space heaters.\n10. Last pass for environmental conditions — mold, pest evidence, trip hazards, sharp edges, and combustible storage.\nTen steps, fifteen to twenty-five minutes per unit once you are practiced, and it is repeatable by a trained employee — which is what makes it a business rather than a job."}]},{"id":"ch10","unit":"u4","number":10,"title":"Inside (Common Area) Deficiencies — Complete Standards","subtitle":"Corridors, stairwells, mechanical rooms and common-area life safety — the densest, cheapest points on any property","sections":[{"heading":"How Inside areas are inspected, and why you start here","body":"Inside covers everything within the building envelope that is not a dwelling unit: corridors, lobbies and vestibules, interior stairwells, elevators and elevator lobbies, community and computer rooms, management offices, mail rooms, laundry rooms, trash rooms and chutes, storage and janitorial closets, boiler and mechanical rooms, electrical rooms, sprinkler riser rooms, fire alarm control panel locations, and elevator machine rooms.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE FIRST DAY OF YOUR ENGAGEMENT\nFour reasons, and they compound:\n\n1. NO SCHEDULING FRICTION. You need no resident notice, no 24-hour entry notice, and no coordination. You can walk every Inside space on the day you arrive.\n2. HIGH SEVERITY, LOW COST. Inside spaces concentrate exactly the findings that carry the most weight and cost the least to clear: open panel ports, blocked egress, combustibles by a boiler, obstructed sprinkler heads, expired extinguisher tags.\n3. THE WEIGHTS ARE NOT MUCH LOWER THAN UNIT. Inside runs about 91 percent of the Unit weight at every severity — 54.5 versus 60.0 at Life-Threatening, 36.4 versus 40.0 at Severe. The idea that \"common areas barely count\" is simply wrong.\n4. IT IS ENTIRELY MANAGEMENT'S DOMAIN. No resident behavior to change, no access to negotiate. Whatever you find can be fixed by the client's own staff, usually the same week.\n\nTHE DUPLICATE RULE, AND WHY IT CUTS BOTH WAYS INSIDE\nRepeat deficiencies within the same inspectable area count once. Inside is a single area covering the whole property, so five propped fire doors in five stairwells collapse to one scored Inside instance. That means:\n• The score exposure from a repeated Inside condition is bounded and modest.\n• Clearing four of the five buys nothing in score terms. Only eliminating the condition entirely removes the deduction.\n• Every instance still carries its own correction obligation.\nSay all three things to the client. A client who hears only the first becomes complacent; one who hears only the second becomes cynical.\n\nTHE INSIDE WALK ORDER\n1. Electrical rooms and every accessible panel.\n2. Boiler and mechanical rooms.\n3. Sprinkler riser room and fire alarm control panel.\n4. Every stairwell, top to bottom, operating every door.\n5. Every corridor, looking for storage, egress obstruction, and lighting.\n6. Laundry, trash and storage rooms.\n7. Community rooms, offices and mail areas.\n8. Elevator lobbies and machine rooms.\nTwo to three hours at a typical mid-size property, and it will routinely produce more correctable points than the first ten units you walk."},{"heading":"Common-area electrical and lighting","body":"THE STANDARD IS THE SAME ONE YOU USE IN UNITS\nElectrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch. Any exposed electrical conductor presenting a hazard: damaged sheathing, an open port, a missing knockout, a missing outlet or switch cover, a missing breaker or fuse, a missing lightbulb leaving an open socket, or AN OPENING OR GAP GREATER THAN 1/2 INCH.\n\nWHERE INSIDE DIFFERS FROM A UNIT\n• PANELS ARE EVERYWHERE AND THEY ARE BIGGER. House panels, distribution panels, subpanels in each stairwell, panels in laundry and mechanical rooms. Open every panel door you are permitted to open, and photograph what you find.\n• EQUIPMENT ROOMS ACCUMULATE DEFECTS BECAUSE NOBODY LIVES THERE. A missing deadfront in an electrical room can sit for years because no resident ever complains about it.\n• ACCESS IS ITS OWN ISSUE. Blocked access to a panel or a disconnect is a deficiency in its own right and also prevents safe emergency response.\n• EMERGENCY AND EXIT LIGHTING. Emergency lights and illuminated exit signs must be present where required and must function. Test them — most units have a test button, and a light that does not illuminate on test does not work in a power failure.\n\nCOMMON-AREA INTERIOR LIGHTING\nInterior lighting is one of the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026. Present-but-inoperable does not satisfy the requirement, and a burned-out fixture in an interior stairwell is both a lighting finding and a genuine hazard on stairs.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the open panel port\nThe main electrical room panel is missing one breaker; the empty position leaves an opening about 1 inch wide with the bus visible.\nAnswer: exposed conductor, Inside area, weight 54.5. At a sample of 12 that is 4.54 points. Correction is a filler plate costing under five dollars. This is, dollar for dollar, one of the highest-return findings you will ever hand a client.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the missing deadfront\nAn electrical room subpanel has its deadfront cover removed and leaning against the wall beside it.\nAnswer: exposed conductor. Note the specific hazard for the client: the cover being present nearby does not mitigate anything, and a maintenance habit of leaving covers off after work is worth addressing at the process level, not just the instance level.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the blocked panel\nA stairwell subpanel is behind stacked furniture and cannot be reached without moving it.\nAnswer: blocked access is a deficiency in its own right. Correction cost is zero. Include an access-clearance line in your standing recommendations, because this recurs continuously.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the emergency light that fails on test\nA corridor emergency light unit has a green power indicator lit. Pressing the test button produces nothing.\nAnswer: the indicator confirms it is receiving power, not that the battery works. A unit that does not illuminate on test does not function. Test every one, and record which ones you tested — a client who can show that every emergency light was tested and dated is in a materially better position than one who cannot.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the dark stairwell\nAn interior stairwell has three fixtures; two are dark.\nAnswer: interior lighting is deficient in a location where the consequence is falls on stairs. It sits in the deferred-scoring group until October 1, 2026, so it is cited and must be corrected but does not deduct before then. Say that clearly and then tell the client to fix it anyway — bulbs are cheap and stair falls are not."},{"heading":"Egress, stairwells, corridors and fire-labeled doors","body":"EGRESS IS THE HIGHEST-YIELD INSIDE INSPECTION\nThe criteria are the same as in units, applied to the building's exit paths:\n• Egress doors must open and close completely and be free of obstructions.\n• Locks requiring a key, tool, or special knowledge or effort from the egress side are prohibited on any door serving as an exit or along the exit access.\n• Exit access — corridors, stairwells, vestibules, and the path to the exterior — must be clear.\nBlocked egress is a top-tier finding.\n\nSTAIRWELLS\n• HANDRAILS ARE REQUIRED WHERE THERE ARE FOUR OR MORE RISERS, must be present on the stair, and must be SECURE.\n• GUARDRAILS ARE REQUIRED WHERE A WALKING SURFACE IS 30 INCHES OR MORE ABOVE GRADE, and must be AT LEAST 30 INCHES IN HEIGHT. Missing, loose, damaged and undersized guardrails are all deficiencies. Guardrail is in the six deferred-scoring categories.\n• Treads and risers must be sound, with no broken, missing, loose or deflecting components.\n• Stairwell doors, lighting and surfaces.\n\nSTORAGE IN EXIT PATHS\nStorage in corridors and stairwells is the single most recurrent Inside finding in the program. It is free to correct, it comes back within weeks, and it is worth building a documented monthly egress walk into your service package for exactly that reason.\n\nFIRE-LABELED DOORS\nFire-labeled doors must be intact, self-closing and self-latching where required, with the label present and legible and no field modifications defeating the rating. Common defects: a failed or disconnected closer, a door held open by a wedge or a hook, a latch that does not engage, a gap that has opened through frame movement, and holes drilled through the door leaf for hardware or signage.\nSCORING NOTE: fire-labeled doors are one of the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements — cited and required to be corrected, not deducting until October 1, 2026.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the wedge\nA stairwell fire door is held open with a rubber wedge on a warm day.\nAnswer: the door cannot self-close, so it cannot perform its function. Cite it. Correction is removing the wedge, and then addressing WHY the wedge exists — usually heat, airflow or a delivery route. If the underlying reason is not addressed, the wedge returns before the next inspection, which is why the recommendation should name the cause.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the propped-versus-failed distinction\nStairwell A's door is propped by a bucket. Stairwell B's door has a closer with a broken arm and drifts to a stop halfway.\nAnswer: both fail to self-close. A is a housekeeping fix; B is a hardware repair. Record them distinctly — a punch list that says \"fire doors\" for both is useless to the person doing the work.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the corridor storage\nA corridor has a resident's bicycle and four stacked boxes narrowing the path to about 30 inches.\nAnswer: obstructed exit access, top-tier egress finding, Inside area at 54.5. Correction is free. Send a notice, remove the items, and put a monthly walk on the calendar.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the chained exit\nA rear exit door has a chain and padlock on the inside, which staff say is removed each morning and replaced at night.\nAnswer: a lock requiring a key from the egress side, on a door serving as an exit. This is among the most serious findings you can make, and the \"we remove it in the morning\" explanation is not a defense — it was in place at the time of observation. Escalate immediately to someone with authority rather than logging it for the report.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the loose stairwell handrail\nA stairwell handrail is present but two brackets have pulled loose and the rail deflects several inches under hand pressure.\nAnswer: a handrail that is not secure does not serve its purpose. Deficiency. Record the deflection you observed and where the brackets failed, so the repair addresses the anchorage rather than just re-tightening the same screws into the same stripped holes."},{"heading":"Mechanical, boiler and electrical rooms","body":"WHAT TO INSPECT IN A MECHANICAL OR BOILER ROOM\n\nCOMBUSTIBLE STORAGE\nCombustibles must not be stored within THREE FEET of a heat appliance or a fuel-burning water heater. Storage on or near ignition sources, and improperly stored chemicals, are deficiencies. In practice, boiler rooms become storage rooms at nearly every property, and this is a zero-cost correction.\n\nCOMBUSTION VENTING\nFlue and chimney piping that is BLOCKED, MISALIGNED, or MISSING — including evidence of prior installation now absent, disconnected pieces, or misalignment at connections along the run — is a deficiency, because that is the path by which combustion gases reach residents rather than the exterior. Sight along every run of flue pipe from appliance to exterior.\n\nFUEL LEAKS\nA natural gas, propane or oil leak is a listed deficiency and belongs at the top of the severity ladder. Smell is the first indicator; escalate immediately rather than continuing your walk.\n\nCARBON MONOXIDE DETECTION\nCO detection is required in the Inside area as well as in units, per chapters 9 and 11 of the 2018 International Fire Code. Statutory, and not subject to any NSPIRE compliance extension.\n\nWATER HEATERS IN COMMON SPACES\nThe same criteria as in a unit: TPR valve active leak or obstruction; discharge piping damaged, capped, upward-sloping or of unsuitable material; discharge terminating outside the 2-TO-6-INCH window above the flood level of the receptor.\n\nELECTRICAL ROOMS\nPanels with covers and deadfronts intact, no open ports, no missing breakers, clear access, and no storage.\n\nSPRINKLER RISER AND FIRE ALARM ROOMS\nRiser and control valves accessible and not obstructed; the fire alarm control panel accessible, not in trouble or silenced condition, and its inspection records available.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the boiler room storage\nA boiler room contains a stack of cardboard boxes about 2 feet from the boiler, a can of paint thinner on a shelf directly above the burner, and a mop and bucket.\nAnswer: the boxes and the thinner are both within three feet of a heat appliance. The thinner above the burner is the more serious placement. The mop and bucket are not combustible storage in the relevant sense and should not be cited — but do note that a boiler room being used as a janitorial closet is how the rest of it starts.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the disconnected flue\nA gas-fired boiler's flue pipe has separated at a joint about four feet above the appliance, leaving a visible gap.\nAnswer: flue misaligned or disconnected. Combustion gases are entering the room. This is an immediate-escalation finding — tell someone with authority before you write it down, and ask whether the appliance should be shut down pending repair.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the CO detector nobody thought about\nA boiler room with two gas-fired boilers and an adjacent community room has no carbon monoxide detection anywhere in the Inside area.\nAnswer: CO detection is required in the Inside area. Statutory, no extension. Correction is inexpensive and the exposure without it is severe.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the common-area water heater\nA mechanical room water heater's TPR discharge pipe terminates 14 inches above the floor drain.\nAnswer: outside the 2-to-6-inch window. Deficiency, Inside area. Same criterion as in a unit; people forget to check the common-area equipment because they associate the standard with dwelling units.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the fire alarm panel in trouble\nThe fire alarm control panel shows an amber trouble light and an audible silence has been engaged. Staff say it has been like that \"for a while.\"\nAnswer: a fire alarm system in a persistent trouble condition is not functioning as designed. Record the panel display verbatim, photograph it, and ask for the most recent inspection, testing and maintenance record. Whether or not you can place the exact citation, the finding, the photograph and the missing service record are what the client needs — and the service record is also what defeats a documentation-based citation elsewhere."},{"heading":"Life safety equipment, laundry, trash and amenity spaces","body":"FIRE EXTINGUISHERS\n• A service tag that is MISSING, ILLEGIBLE, or EXPIRED — where the date on the tag has exceeded ONE YEAR — is a deficiency.\n• EXCLUSION: if the owner provides an invoice or report from the servicing fire extinguisher company, a missing-tag deficiency should not be recorded, provided the report date is NO MORE THAN ONE YEAR from the inspection date.\n• Also check: extinguisher present in its designated location, mounted, unobstructed, gauge in the operable range, pin and seal intact, and no visible damage.\n\nSPRINKLER ASSEMBLIES\n• An assembly ENCASED OR OBSTRUCTED by an item or object WITHIN 18 INCHES of the sprinkler head is a deficiency.\n• EXCLUSIONS: heads that lack 18 inches of clearance because of permanent features of the built environment — a closet, wall-mounted kitchen cabinets, a permanently installed light fixture, an exit sign — are not deficiencies, and neither is a parked vehicle within 18 inches.\n• Also check: painted, corroded, damaged or missing heads; missing escutcheons; and hanging items on heads or piping, which is a recurring find in storage and laundry rooms.\n\nFIRE ALARM SYSTEMS\nDevices present and undamaged, the control panel accessible and not in a persistent trouble or silenced condition, and inspection, testing and maintenance records available. Keep the records in the certificate file described in Chapter 8.\n\nSMOKE AND CO ALARMS IN COMMON AREAS\nThe Inside area must include at least one battery-operated or hard-wired smoke detector in proper working condition on each level of the property, and CO detection per the 2018 IFC.\n\nCALL-FOR-AID SYSTEMS\nCall-for-aid systems — used by a resident to summon help in a medical emergency, either alerting an external response source or providing internal notification with alarms and lights — are NOT REQUIRED under the inspection protocol. If a property has one, it should function, but the absence of a system is not a deficiency. Knowing this saves clients from being told by less careful consultants that they need to install one.\n\nLAUNDRY ROOMS\nDryer venting to the exterior and lint accumulation; appliance condition; electrical devices and cover plates; GFCI protection within 6 feet of a water source; floor drains; trip hazards; and sprinkler clearance, since laundry rooms accumulate stored items.\n\nTRASH ROOMS AND CHUTES\nChute doors that close and latch, sanitation, pest evidence, and combustible accumulation.\n\nCOMMUNITY ROOMS, OFFICES AND MAIL AREAS\nStandard electrical, egress, surfaces and lighting criteria. Kitchenettes in community rooms carry the same appliance, plumbing and GFCI criteria as a unit kitchen.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the tag versus the invoice\nExtinguisher A has a tag dated 14 months ago. Extinguisher B has no tag, and the manager produces a service company invoice dated four months ago covering all extinguishers.\nAnswer: A is a deficiency — the tag date has exceeded one year. B is NOT a deficiency — the invoice within one year satisfies the exclusion. Two extinguishers, opposite answers, and the difference is a piece of paper. This is why the certificate and service file is a product.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — reading the sprinkler exclusions\nA storage room has boxes stacked to within 10 inches of a head. A corridor has an exit sign 8 inches from a head. A laundry room has a coat hanger looped over a head.\nAnswer: the boxes are a deficiency. The exit sign is excluded as a permanent built-environment feature. The coat hanger on the head is a deficiency and a serious one — anything hanging from a head risks accidental discharge and defeats the spray pattern.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the painted head\nA basement corridor sprinkler head has been overspray-painted during a repaint.\nAnswer: a painted head is a damaged assembly — paint insulates the thermal element and delays or prevents activation. Cite it and have it replaced, not cleaned.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the laundry dryer vent\nA common laundry room's dryer vent duct has separated behind the machine; lint has accumulated on the floor and wall behind it.\nAnswer: the venting is not discharging to the exterior and lint is accumulating near a heat-producing appliance. Record both the separated duct and the combustible accumulation. Cheap fix, real fire risk, and it recurs — put lint and duct checks on the client's monthly schedule.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the call-for-aid question\nA manager asks whether their 1980s building needs a call-for-aid system installed to pass NSPIRE, having been told by another consultant that it does.\nAnswer: no. Call-for-aid systems are not required under the inspection protocol. If the building HAS one, it should work. Telling a client confidently that they do not need to spend money is one of the fastest ways to earn their trust — and, in this case, it is simply correct."}]},{"id":"ch11","unit":"u4","number":11,"title":"Outside Deficiencies — Complete Standards","subtitle":"Site and building envelope — where the cheap points are, where the systemic failures start, and where clients waste money","sections":[{"heading":"What Outside covers and how to think about it","body":"Outside covers the exterior of the building and everything on the grounds.\n\nBUILDING EXTERIOR COMPONENTS: roofs and roof drainage, exterior walls and cladding, foundations, exterior doors and windows, attached porches, attached patios, balconies, carports, fire escapes, and lighting mounted on the building.\n\nSITE COMPONENTS: walkways and sidewalks, parking lots, driveways, roads, storm drainage, site and pole lighting, fencing and gates, retaining walls, mailboxes, project signs, refuse disposal areas and enclosures, play areas and playground equipment, detached garages, and other non-dwelling buildings.\n\nTHE WEIGHTS\nOutside runs about 83 percent of the Unit weight at every severity level: 49.6 at Life-Threatening, 33.1 at Severe, 16.5 at Moderate, 2.0 at Low. That is lower than Unit, but it is not trivial, and the widespread belief that \"outside doesn't count\" is simply an arithmetic error.\n\nTHREE THINGS TO GET RIGHT ABOUT OUTSIDE\n\n1. IT IS THE CHEAPEST POINTS-PER-DOLLAR ON THE PROPERTY, BUT ONLY FOR REAL DEFICIENCIES. Grinding a sidewalk lift, replacing a pole lamp, moving stored combustibles — small deductions, tiny costs, excellent ratios. Repainting the sign and mulching the beds — no deductions at all, real money spent. The difference between those two lists is what the client is paying you to know.\n\n2. IT IS WHERE SYSTEMIC UNIT FAILURES ORIGINATE. A failing roof, failed flashing, blocked gutters, or grading that drives water at a foundation produces water intrusion, which produces mold-like substance in units, scored at the highest area weight with escalating severity. When you find an envelope failure, you have usually found the root of a cluster of Unit findings, and that is how it should be presented.\n\n3. LIFE-THREATENING FINDINGS LIVE OUT HERE TOO. A collapsing stair, a missing guardrail on an elevated walkway, an exposed conductor at an exterior fixture, a sinkhole in a walking path. Low area weight does not mean low severity, and the 24-hour clock does not care where the finding was.\n\nTHE OUTSIDE WALK ORDER\n1. Walk the full perimeter of every building, looking up at the roof edge, gutters and flashing and down at grade and foundation.\n2. Walk every exterior stair, landing, walkway, balcony and fire escape, measuring rails and level differentials.\n3. Walk every walking path with a ruler, checking vertical and horizontal differentials.\n4. Check every exterior light fixture and every pole light — and return after dark if you can, because a fixture that looks fine at noon may not illuminate.\n5. Check refuse areas, playgrounds, fencing, retaining walls, mailboxes and signage.\n6. Note the parking areas, drainage and any standing water."},{"heading":"Walking surfaces, trip hazards and site circulation","body":"STANDARD: TRIP HAZARD\nThe measurable criteria:\n• VERTICAL: a difference of 3/4 INCH in the walking path.\n• HORIZONTAL: a separation of at least 2 INCHES.\nInspect along the normal paths of travel.\n\nWHERE THEY ACCUMULATE\nSidewalk joints lifted by tree roots; the transition from walkway to parking lot; settled slabs at building entries; the lip at a curb ramp; expansion joints that have opened; broken or missing sections of paving; and the edge where an asphalt patch meets concrete.\n\nSTANDARD: SHARP EDGES\nA sharp edge on or adjacent to the built environment that can result in a cut or puncture hazard LIKELY TO REQUIRE EMERGENCY CARE — for example, stitches. Inspect along normal paths of travel including sidewalks, walkways, playgrounds and courtyards. The test is the severity of the injury the edge could cause, not merely that the edge is rough.\n\nOTHER SITE CIRCULATION CONDITIONS\nHoles, depressions and sinkholes in walking paths; standing water on walkways; failed or missing handrails and guardrails on exterior stairs; deteriorated stair treads; and obstructed paths.\n\nEXTERIOR STAIRS AND RAILINGS\n• A HANDRAIL IS REQUIRED WHERE THERE ARE FOUR OR MORE RISERS.\n• A GUARDRAIL IS REQUIRED WHERE THE WALKING SURFACE IS 30 INCHES OR MORE ABOVE GRADE, and where required it must be AT LEAST 30 INCHES IN HEIGHT. Missing, loose, damaged and undersized guardrails are all deficiencies.\n• Guardrail is one of the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements — cited and corrected now, deducting from October 1, 2026.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — three sidewalk locations\nLocation A: a lifted joint measuring 1/2 inch. Location B: a lifted joint measuring 1 inch. Location C: an expansion joint that has opened to a 3-inch gap.\nAnswer: A is under the 3/4-inch vertical criterion and is not a deficiency. B exceeds it and is. C exceeds the 2-inch horizontal criterion and is. Two findings from three locations, decided entirely by a ruler.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the elevated walkway\nA second-floor exterior walkway serving four units sits about 11 feet above grade. The guardrail measures 29 inches high.\nAnswer: a guardrail is required, and at 29 inches it is undersized against the 30-inch minimum. Deficiency, in the deferred-scoring group. Measure rail height at several points — rails are frequently compliant at one end and not the other because of deck slope.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — counting exterior risers\nAn exterior stair from grade to a first-floor entry has three risers and no handrail. A second stair to a basement entry has five risers and no handrail.\nAnswer: the three-riser stair does not trigger the requirement. The five-riser stair does. Count, do not estimate.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the sharp edge test\nA chain-link fence has a cut top rail with a protruding spike at chest height beside a walkway. Elsewhere, a concrete curb has a chipped edge with a rough surface.\nAnswer: the protruding spike at chest height beside a path of travel plausibly causes a puncture requiring emergency care — that meets the criterion. The chipped curb is rough but is not going to cause an injury requiring stitches. Cite the first, not the second, and describe what you saw precisely enough that the reasoning is visible.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the collapsing stair\nA wooden exterior stair serving two second-floor units has a stringer split through its depth, and the treads deflect visibly under load.\nAnswer: a collapse and fall hazard, top-tier, Outside area at 49.6. Immediate action: close the stair, provide alternate access, repair. Note separately whether a guardrail is also missing alongside it, since that is a distinct finding in the deferred-scoring group."},{"heading":"Building envelope — roof, walls, foundation, windows and doors","body":"ROOFS AND ROOF DRAINAGE\nWhat to look for from grade and from any accessible vantage: missing, lifted or displaced roofing material; ponding; damaged or missing flashing at penetrations, valleys, parapets and wall intersections; gutters and downspouts that are missing, detached, blocked or discharging against the building; and staining on exterior walls below roof edges, which is the visible signature of a drainage failure.\n\nEXTERIOR WALLS AND CLADDING\nMissing, damaged or displaced siding, brick, stucco or panel; cracks that penetrate the cladding; open joints and failed sealant at penetrations and around openings; damaged soffits and fascia; and deteriorated painted surfaces, which in pre-1978 housing carry lead-safe-work-practice implications that should be flagged to the client alongside the NSPIRE finding.\n\nFOUNDATIONS\nCracks, spalling, displacement, and evidence of settlement. Grading that slopes toward the building rather than away from it. Blocked or missing foundation vents where present. Water staining or efflorescence at the base of walls.\n\nEXTERIOR DOORS AND WINDOWS\n• Doors that do not open, close or latch; damaged frames; failed weatherstripping allowing water or air intrusion; and doors that cannot be secured, which sits in the security family at Severe.\n• Windows with broken, cracked or missing glazing; failed seals; sashes that will not operate; and missing or broken hardware.\n• Building entry doors that do not latch or lock, leaving the building open, are security findings.\n\nBALCONIES, PORCHES, PATIOS, CARPORTS AND FIRE ESCAPES\nStructural condition of decks, framing and connections; guardrails against the 30-inch criteria; deteriorated surfaces; and for fire escapes, whether the assembly is intact, accessible and unobstructed. Fire escape access obstruction is an egress finding.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — following the water\nA unit reports ceiling staining in a top-floor bedroom. From outside, you see a downspout that has detached at the gutter and is discharging directly onto the roof deck at a valley, and staining on the exterior wall below.\nAnswer: an Outside drainage finding that is the root of a Unit-area condition. Present it as one repair retiring a cluster. Then measure any mold-like substance inside against the 4-square-inch, 1-square-foot and 9-square-foot thresholds, because that is what determines the severity of the Unit findings.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the grading problem\nThe grade along the north side of building B slopes toward the foundation; there is standing water at the base of the wall and efflorescence on the interior of the adjacent basement storage room.\nAnswer: record the exterior grading and drainage condition and the interior evidence. This is a case where the honest advice is that the interior symptom cannot be fixed without the exterior cause, and where a client will otherwise spend repeatedly on repainting a wall that will fail again.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the entry door that will not latch\nA building's rear common entry door has a failed closer; the door drifts and does not latch, leaving the building accessible.\nAnswer: a security finding under the Severe language. The part costs very little. Note where the standard places the component and record the location precisely.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the cracked window\nA ground-floor window has a crack across one pane; the pane is intact and the window operates and locks. A second window has a missing pane covered with cardboard and tape.\nAnswer: the cardboard-covered opening is plainly a deficiency — the building envelope is breached and the window cannot be secured. The single cracked pane requires judgment: describe it precisely, note whether the glazing is loose or the crack is through the pane, and let the criterion decide. Do not put a client to the expense of replacing sound glazing on your hunch.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the fire escape\nA fire escape's lower drop ladder is secured with a padlock, and a resident's storage bin sits on the second-floor landing.\nAnswer: two egress obstructions on the same assembly. Both are top-tier egress findings, and both are free to correct. Fire escapes are among the most frequently overlooked Outside components because they are rarely used, which is exactly why obstructions accumulate on them."},{"heading":"Site systems, lighting, refuse and amenities","body":"EXTERIOR AND SITE LIGHTING\nThe standard covers permanently installed light fixtures on the building exterior or on the site and grounds — walkway lighting, pole lighting, wall packs, canopy lights — that illuminate exterior areas such as entryways, parking lots and exterior stairwells.\nWhat to check: fixtures present where required, operating, undamaged, with intact lenses and housings, and no exposed conductors at damaged fixtures or junctions. A damaged exterior fixture with visible wiring is an exposed-conductor finding at the top of the ladder, not a lighting nuisance.\nPractical method: photograph every fixture during the day and return after dark to confirm which ones actually illuminate. Nothing else tells you reliably.\n\nFENCING, GATES AND RETAINING WALLS\nFencing damaged, leaning, or with sharp protrusions; gates that do not operate or secure; and retaining walls with cracking, bulging, displacement, or evidence of failure. A retaining wall showing bulging or displacement above a walking path is a genuine structural hazard and should be escalated rather than logged.\n\nREFUSE DISPOSAL\nEnclosure condition, gates, sanitation, overflow, and pest evidence. Refuse areas are where infestation citations originate, and where the evidence-based infestation criteria — droppings, shed skins, egg cases 5 to 9 millimeters — are easiest to find.\n\nPLAY AREAS AND PLAYGROUND EQUIPMENT\nEquipment condition, damaged or missing components, protrusions and sharp edges against the emergency-care criterion, surfacing condition, and entrapment or fall hazards. Where equipment is unsafe and cannot be immediately repaired, removal or securing the area is the correct interim step.\n\nPARKING, DRIVES AND STORM DRAINAGE\nPaving condition where it affects walking paths; drainage inlets blocked or damaged; standing water; and striping and accessible-space condition where local requirements apply.\n\nMAILBOXES AND SIGNAGE\nDamaged or unsecured mailboxes; missing or illegible building and unit identification, which matters for emergency response.\n\nEXTERIOR ELECTRICAL AND METERS\nMeter bases, disconnects, exterior panels and junction boxes: covers intact, no open ports, no exposed conductors, and no blocked access.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the wall pack\nA wall pack at a building entry has a cracked housing with the wiring splice visible and weather-exposed.\nAnswer: exposed conductor, Outside area at 49.6. Do not treat this as a lighting item — the exposed conductor is the finding, and at Outside weight it is not far below what the same condition would cost inside a unit.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the pole lights\nA property has fourteen pole lights. At 2 p.m. all fourteen look intact. Returning at 9 p.m., four do not illuminate.\nAnswer: four site lighting findings you could not have made in daylight. Within the Outside area the duplicate rule collapses them to one scored instance, but all four are safety and security conditions worth correcting, and the after-dark visit is what made the finding possible. Build it into your standard engagement.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the retaining wall\nA four-foot retaining wall above a resident walkway shows a horizontal crack and about an inch of outward bulge at mid-height.\nAnswer: evidence of structural failure above a path of travel. Escalate immediately, recommend the path be closed pending evaluation, and recommend an engineer rather than attempting to classify the repair yourself. Knowing the limit of your own competence is part of the professional standard.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the refuse enclosure\nA trash enclosure has a gate off its hinge, overflowing containers, and visible rodent droppings and burrows at the base of the enclosure wall.\nAnswer: multiple findings — the damaged enclosure gate, the sanitation condition, and infestation evidence in the Outside area. Note that if pest evidence also appears in units and common areas, the extent escalation applies and the condition moves to the top tier property-wide.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the playground\nA playground has a slide with a cracked panel leaving a jagged plastic edge at hand height, and one swing seat missing with the chains hanging.\nAnswer: assess the jagged edge against the sharp-edge criterion — a cut or puncture likely to require emergency care — and record the damaged and missing equipment. Where equipment is unsafe and cannot be repaired promptly, securing or removing it is the correct interim step, and it should be documented as an interim measure with a scheduled permanent repair."},{"heading":"Where clients waste money outside, and what to tell them","body":"Every property manager you meet has an instinct about what makes a property look inspection-ready, and the instinct is almost entirely about the Outside area. Correcting it politely and specifically is one of the fastest ways to demonstrate that you know something they do not.\n\nTHE FOUR CLASSIC MISALLOCATIONS\n\n1. LANDSCAPING AND MULCH. Unless overgrowth is actually obstructing a walking path, blocking a light fixture, or growing into the building envelope, landscaping is not a scored condition. It is the single largest category of pre-inspection spending that buys nothing.\n\n2. REPAINTING SIGNAGE AND STRIPING. Signage matters for legibility and emergency response; a faded-but-legible sign is not a deficiency. Parking striping is not a scored condition except where local accessibility requirements are implicated.\n\n3. PRESSURE WASHING AND GENERAL TIDYING. Litter and grime are not deficiencies. Grime concealing a genuine defect is a different matter, and cleaning that reveals a defect is useful — but the cleaning itself buys no points.\n\n4. SEALCOATING PARKING LOTS. A large expense, and pavement condition matters mainly where it affects a walking path or drainage. Sealcoating a sound lot is a maintenance decision, not an inspection decision.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY BUYS POINTS OUTSIDE, RANKED\n\n1. ANY EXPOSED CONDUCTOR AT AN EXTERIOR FIXTURE, METER BASE, DISCONNECT OR JUNCTION. Top of the ladder at 49.6, and the correction is usually a cover or a housing.\n2. EGRESS OBSTRUCTIONS — fire escapes, exterior exit paths, gates on required exit routes. Free to correct, top tier.\n3. FALL AND COLLAPSE HAZARDS — failing exterior stairs, deteriorated landings, failing retaining walls above walkways.\n4. TRIP HAZARDS OVER 3/4 INCH VERTICAL OR 2 INCHES HORIZONTAL. Grinding is inexpensive and the findings are unambiguous.\n5. NON-FUNCTIONING SITE AND EXTERIOR LIGHTING. Usually lamps and photocells.\n6. GUARDRAILS AND HANDRAILS AGAINST THE MEASURED CRITERIA. Currently in the deferred-scoring group, so frame this as getting ahead of October 1, 2026.\n7. ENVELOPE FAILURES DRIVING INTERIOR CONDITIONS. Rarely cheap, but the highest leverage on the property, because the repair retires Unit-area findings at the highest weights.\n\nTHE CONVERSATION TO HAVE\nGive the client the two lists side by side, with the estimated cost of each item and the estimated points recovered. Then say the sentence that closes the argument:\n\n\"Everything on the left costs money and recovers no points. Everything on the right costs less in total and recovers real points. If you do nothing else from this report, move the budget from the left column to the right one.\"\n\nTwo cautions so you stay honest:\n• Do not tell a client that appearance is worthless. It matters for residents, for lease-up, and for the tone an inspector arrives in. It is simply not SCORED, and that distinction is the one you are being paid to draw.\n• Do not present your point estimates as certainties. Give ranges, state your assumed sample size, and note which items sit in the deferred-scoring group and therefore recover nothing before October 1, 2026. A ranked list with honest uncertainty is worth more to a client than a confident number that turns out wrong."}]},{"id":"ch12","unit":"u5","number":12,"title":"Elevator Standards & Certificates","subtitle":"What NSPIRE actually cites in an elevator, the certificate rule that surprises everyone, and where jurisdiction takes over","sections":[{"heading":"The certificate rule — read this before anything else","body":"Start with the fact that most consultants get wrong, because getting it right is worth money to a client and getting it wrong costs you credibility.\n\nUNDER NSPIRE, THE ELEVATOR CERTIFICATE IS VERIFIED DURING THE INSPECTION — BUT NO DEFICIENCY IS RECORDED FOR EITHER FAILURE TO PROVIDE A CERTIFICATE OR AN EXPIRED CERTIFICATE.\n\nRead it twice. The inspector will ask for the certificate. Images of certificates provided to the inspector appear in the inspection report. But the absence of the certificate, and an expired certificate, are not themselves recorded deficiencies under the inspection protocol.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN\n\nIt does NOT mean elevator certificates do not matter. It means the consequence does not arrive through the NSPIRE score. It arrives through:\n• STATE AND LOCAL LAW. Nearly every jurisdiction requires periodic elevator inspection and a current, posted operating certificate. The interval, the inspecting authority, and the posting requirement all VARY BY JURISDICTION. Operating an elevator on an expired certificate is a local code violation with its own penalties, and in some jurisdictions it can result in the unit being taken out of service.\n• INSURANCE AND CONTRACT. Carriers and maintenance contracts routinely require current certification.\n• LIABILITY. In any injury claim, an expired certificate is the first document opposing counsel asks for.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS TO YOUR PRACTICE\nTwo concrete uses:\n1. TECHNICAL REVIEW. If an inspector records a deficiency for a missing or expired elevator certificate, that is a clean criteria-not-met filing under the inspector-error ground. Specific, checkable, and the kind of detail that pays for your fee on its own.\n2. HONEST ADVICE. When a client is panicking about an expired certificate two weeks before an inspection, you can tell them the truth: it is not a scored deficiency, and separately it is a local compliance problem they need to solve on its own timeline. That combination — accurate on the score, serious about the underlying obligation — is what a good advisor sounds like.\n\nVerify this against the current published elevator standard for the applicable program before you rely on it in a filing. It is a specific rule that could be revised, and a filing built on a superseded rule is worse than no filing."},{"heading":"What NSPIRE actually evaluates in an elevator","body":"THE STANDARD: ELEVATOR\nThe recorded deficiencies center on whether the elevator functions safely as a conveyance. The listed conditions include:\n\n• THE ELEVATOR IS INOPERABLE.\n• THE DOORS DO NOT FULLY OPEN AND CLOSE.\n• THE CAB IS NOT LEVEL WITH THE FLOOR — the criterion is a difference of MORE THAN 3/4 INCH between the elevator car and the adjoining floor. Put another way, there should not be a 3/4-inch or greater difference between the car and the landing.\n• THE SAFETY EDGE DEVICE HAS MALFUNCTIONED OR IS INOPERABLE. This is the door-reversing device — the mechanical safety edge or the electronic detector edge that causes the doors to reopen when they encounter an obstruction.\n\nHOW TO TEST, IN ORDER\n1. RIDE IT TO EVERY LANDING. Not just the lobby and one floor — every landing. Mis-leveling is frequently landing-specific.\n2. MEASURE THE LEVEL AT EACH LANDING. Use a ruler or a small rule laid across the sill gap. Record the number at each landing. Photograph the measurement at any landing over the criterion.\n3. TEST THE SAFETY EDGE AT EACH LANDING. Obstruct the closing doors with a soft object — a rolled towel, a notebook, never a hand or foot — and confirm the doors reverse.\n4. OPERATE THE DOORS FULLY. Confirm full open and full close, no binding, no partial travel, no dragging.\n5. CHECK THE CAB INTERIOR. Lighting, emergency communication device, floor covering condition, ventilation, and legible floor indication.\n6. NOTE THE CERTIFICATE. Photograph it, record the date, and record its absence if it is not posted — as information for the client and their local compliance obligation, not as a scored NSPIRE deficiency.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — measuring across the landings\nA four-stop elevator. You measure the level difference at each landing: lobby flush, second floor 1/4 inch low, third floor 1 inch low, fourth floor 1/2 inch high.\nAnswer: only the third floor exceeds the 3/4-inch criterion and is a deficiency. Record the measurement at every landing anyway — the pattern tells the elevator contractor something, and the documented numbers protect the client if the condition is later disputed.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the safety edge that half works\nThe doors reverse when the mechanical edge is pressed, but the electronic detector beam does not reverse them when interrupted.\nAnswer: a safety edge device that has malfunctioned. Record which device failed and how you tested it. The distinction matters to the contractor and to the credibility of your report.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the intermittently inoperable elevator\nStaff report that the elevator \"sometimes stops between floors\" and has been reset three times this month. During your visit it operates normally.\nAnswer: you cannot cite a condition you did not observe. What you CAN do is record the reported history, request the maintenance log and the last inspection report, and put a written recommendation in your report that the intermittent fault be diagnosed before the inspection window. That is the honest and useful response, and it is also what a client remembers when the elevator strands someone.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the doors that bind\nThe doors close fully but bind and drag on the last few inches at one landing, opening and closing noticeably slower there.\nAnswer: the doors do open and close, so the \"do not fully open and close\" criterion is not squarely met — but binding is a precursor to failure and to a safety edge fault. Describe the observation precisely, note the landing, and recommend service. Do not manufacture a citation; do give the client the information.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the cab with no working phone\nThe cab's emergency communication device produces no dial tone and no response when pressed.\nAnswer: an inoperable emergency communication device in an elevator is a serious condition and is separately a requirement in most jurisdictions. Record it precisely and escalate it. Where you cannot place it under a specific NSPIRE deficiency, say so in your report and cite the local requirement — a report that distinguishes \"NSPIRE deficiency\" from \"code and safety issue you must still fix\" is more useful than one that blurs them."},{"heading":"Machine rooms and pits","body":"The elevator machine room and the pit are Inside-area spaces and are inspected under the same standards that apply to any other building system space — plus the elevator-specific conditions that a competent inspector and a competent consultant will look for.\n\nMACHINE ROOM — WHAT TO CHECK\n• ACCESS. The room must be accessible and the door secured against unauthorized entry. Blocked access is a deficiency in its own right.\n• LIGHTING. Working permanent lighting.\n• TEMPERATURE. Machine rooms have equipment temperature limits; an overheating room causes shutdowns. Note excessive heat and the absence or failure of ventilation or cooling.\n• NO UNRELATED STORAGE. Elevator machine rooms are not storage rooms. Stored material is a combustible and access problem and, where it sits near a heat source, meets the three-foot combustible criterion.\n• ELECTRICAL. Panels and disconnects with covers intact, no open ports, no exposed conductors, clear access, and proper labeling of the elevator disconnect.\n• HOUSEKEEPING AND LEAKS. Oil accumulation, hydraulic leaks, and water intrusion.\n\nPIT — WHAT TO CHECK\n• CLEAN AND DRY. Standing water in a pit is common and it is a genuine problem — it corrodes equipment and creates a shock hazard around pit wiring.\n• PIT LIGHT AND STOP SWITCH. Both present and functioning.\n• BUFFER CONDITION. Buffers intact and properly serviced.\n• NO DEBRIS OR STORED MATERIAL.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the machine room as storage\nAn elevator machine room contains the controller, a stack of paint cans, three broken chairs and a box of holiday decorations.\nAnswer: unrelated storage in an elevator machine room. Assess the paint cans against the three-foot combustible criterion relative to any heat source, and record the access obstruction separately if the storage blocks the controller or the disconnect. Correction cost is zero and this recurs at nearly every property.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the flooded pit\nThe elevator pit has approximately two inches of standing water, with the pit light fixture mounted about a foot above the water line and visible corrosion on the buffer bases.\nAnswer: record the water depth, the corrosion, and the proximity of electrical components. Determine the source — groundwater intrusion, a plumbing leak, or drainage failure — because a pit pumped without addressing the source refills. Escalate: standing water around energized pit equipment is not a defer-to-the-report finding.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the machine room that is too hot\nA machine room registers noticeably hot with no ventilation running; staff report summer shutdowns.\nAnswer: an environmental condition that will produce elevator failures. Record the observation and the reported shutdown history, and recommend evaluation of the room's ventilation or cooling. This is preventive advice rather than a citation, and it is the kind of finding that makes a client renew.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the unlabeled disconnect\nThe machine room has three disconnects, none labeled.\nAnswer: labeling matters for emergency response and for safe service. Record it. Where you cannot place it under a specific NSPIRE deficiency, note it as a code and safety recommendation and be clear about which it is.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the blocked machine room door\nThe machine room door is accessible but a maintenance cart and a ladder are stored directly against it in the corridor.\nAnswer: blocked access. Free to correct. Also note the corridor storage separately if it obstructs the exit access, because that is a top-tier egress finding rather than a housekeeping note."},{"heading":"Jurisdiction, contracts, and what varies","body":"Elevators are the clearest example in this entire course of a system where NSPIRE is only part of the regulatory picture. Say this to clients explicitly, because it prevents both complacency and panic.\n\nWHAT VARIES BY JURISDICTION — assume nothing and verify locally\n• INSPECTION INTERVAL. Periodic inspection requirements are set by state or local authority and vary. Some jurisdictions require annual inspection; others differ by equipment type or building occupancy.\n• WHO INSPECTS. Depending on the jurisdiction, elevator inspections may be performed by a state or municipal inspector, by a licensed third-party inspector, or by a qualified elevator inspector engaged by the owner.\n• CERTIFICATE POSTING. Many jurisdictions require the current operating certificate to be posted in the cab or at a specified location. Posting requirements, format and duration vary.\n• TESTING REQUIREMENTS. Periodic safety tests — for example category testing of safeties, governors and buffers — are set by code and by adopted national standards, and the required intervals vary by jurisdiction and equipment type.\n• PENALTIES AND ENFORCEMENT. Ranging from fines to orders taking the elevator out of service.\n\nBecause all of this varies, the only defensible thing to write in a client report is what you have actually verified for that jurisdiction. Two sentences that belong in every elevator section you write:\n\"Elevator inspection intervals, certificate posting requirements and periodic testing requirements are set by state and local authority and vary by jurisdiction. The requirements applicable to this property should be confirmed with the local authority having jurisdiction.\"\n\nTHE MAINTENANCE FILE YOU SHOULD BUILD FOR EVERY CLIENT WITH AN ELEVATOR\nEven though the certificate is not a scored NSPIRE deficiency, the file is worth assembling:\n• The CURRENT OPERATING CERTIFICATE, photographed, with its expiration date and a calendar reminder at 60 days out.\n• The MOST RECENT INSPECTION REPORT, including any open items and their status.\n• The CURRENT MAINTENANCE CONTRACT, with the scope, the response-time commitment, and the after-hours contact.\n• RECENT SERVICE AND CALLBACK RECORDS. A pattern of callbacks for the same fault is the single best predictor of a failure during an inspection window, and it is an argument for pressing the contractor.\n• PERIODIC TEST RECORDS as required locally.\n\nTHE CODE-CONFLICT GROUND\nRecall from Chapter 8 that conflict with state or local code is one of the four grounds for technical review. Elevators, boilers, sprinklers and fire alarms are where this ground actually comes up, because these are the systems where local jurisdictions impose their own inspection, permitting and certificate regimes. If a cited condition exists because a local requirement compelled it, that is a filing — and it requires the code citation and, ideally, a letter from the local authority having jurisdiction.\n\nWHEN TO CALL AN ELEVATOR CONTRACTOR RATHER THAN WRITING A LINE ITEM\nImmediately, not at the end of the engagement, for any of these:\n• Standing water in the pit with energized equipment nearby.\n• A safety edge device that does not reverse the doors.\n• Mis-leveling over the 3/4-inch criterion, particularly if it varies between visits.\n• Any report of the car stopping between floors or of passengers being stranded.\n• An inoperable emergency communication device.\nKnowing which findings you escalate and which you log is a large part of what distinguishes a professional from a checklist walker."},{"heading":"The elevator section of your report","body":"A worked template you can lift directly. The point of the structure is that it separates three things clients constantly conflate: what NSPIRE will score, what local code requires, and what will simply break.\n\nSECTION 1 — NSPIRE-SCORED FINDINGS\nOnly conditions meeting a recorded NSPIRE deficiency. For each: the standard name, the location, the measurement where one applies, a photograph, and the point effect at the assumed sample size.\nExample line: \"Elevator — cab not level with floor. Landing: 3rd floor. Measured 1 inch below landing (criterion: more than 3/4 inch). Photograph 14. Inside area, weight applied at assumed sample of 16.\"\n\nSECTION 2 — NOT NSPIRE-SCORED, BUT REQUIRED\nConditions that are real obligations arriving through a channel other than the score. This is where the certificate goes.\nExample line: \"Elevator operating certificate expired 4 March. NOT a recorded NSPIRE deficiency — the certificate is verified during inspection but neither its absence nor its expiration is itself a recorded deficiency. It IS a local compliance obligation; confirm the requirement and the renewal process with the authority having jurisdiction. Recommend renewal before the inspection window.\"\n\nSECTION 3 — RELIABILITY AND PREVENTIVE OBSERVATIONS\nConditions that are not citations today and are likely to become failures. Machine room heat, door binding, callback patterns, pit water.\nExample line: \"Machine room ambient temperature high with no operating ventilation; staff report two summer shutdowns. Not a citation. Recommend evaluation of machine room cooling before the summer inspection window.\"\n\nSECTION 4 — ESCALATIONS ALREADY MADE\nAnything you reported verbally on site to someone with authority, with the time, the name and what was said. This protects the client and it protects you.\n\nWHY THE THREE-SECTION SPLIT MATTERS\nA client reading a single undifferentiated list has no way to allocate money or urgency. The split lets them do exactly that: Section 1 is scored and belongs in the score projection, Section 2 is compliance work with its own deadline, Section 3 is capital and maintenance planning. It also protects you — when the certificate does not appear on the final report as a deficiency, your report already said it would not, which is a small moment that buys a lot of trust.\n\nTwo habits to close on:\n• Photograph the certificate every visit and diary the expiration at 60 days. It costs nothing and clients consistently forget.\n• Never write \"elevator OK.\" Write what you tested, at which landings, and what you measured. A report that shows the method is worth several times one that shows only the conclusion."}]},{"id":"ch13","unit":"u5","number":13,"title":"Boiler, HVAC & Fire System Standards","subtitle":"Central heating, unit HVAC, sprinklers, extinguishers and fire alarms — NSPIRE criteria, certificate regimes, and what varies locally","sections":[{"heading":"Boilers and central heating","body":"WHAT NSPIRE EVALUATES\nCentral heating equipment is inspected under the same body of standards that applies to any building system in the Inside area, with three families doing most of the work:\n\n1. COMBUSTION VENTING. Flue and chimney piping that is BLOCKED, MISALIGNED, or MISSING — including evidence of prior installation now absent, disconnected pieces, or misalignment at connections along the run — is a deficiency, because that is the path by which combustion gases reach residents rather than the exterior. Sight along the full run from appliance to exterior; the failures are usually at joints and at penetrations.\n\n2. FUEL LEAKS. A natural gas, propane or oil leak is a listed deficiency at the top of the severity ladder. Odor is the first indicator. Escalate immediately rather than continuing your walk.\n\n3. COMBUSTIBLE STORAGE. Combustibles must not be stored WITHIN THREE FEET of a heat appliance or a fuel-burning water heater. Boiler rooms become storage rooms at nearly every property.\n\nAlongside those, apply the ordinary Inside criteria to the room itself: electrical panels and disconnects with covers intact and no open ports, clear access, working lighting, and carbon monoxide detection, which is required in the Inside area and is statutory with no NSPIRE extension.\n\nTHE PERFORMANCE CRITERION\nCentral heating exists to meet the HVAC criterion at the unit: when the OUTSIDE temperature is BELOW 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT, the resident must be able to maintain a MINIMUM OF 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT inside through a safe heating source. A central plant that cannot deliver that to the units it serves produces unit-level findings even if the boiler room itself looks immaculate.\nSCORING NOTE: HVAC is one of the six affirmative requirements deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026 — cited and required to be corrected, not deducting before that date.\n\nBOILER CERTIFICATES — A JURISDICTIONAL REGIME, NOT A NSPIRE ONE\nBoiler inspection and certification is governed by STATE OR LOCAL law, not by the NSPIRE standards. The requirements vary substantially:\n• The general pattern in many states is that high-pressure power boilers receive a certificate inspection ANNUALLY, while low-pressure heating boilers and hot water heating or supply boilers receive one BIENNIALLY — but this is a pattern, not a rule, and the interval is set by the jurisdiction.\n• Which authority inspects — a state boiler inspector, a municipal inspector, or an authorized insurance-company inspector — varies.\n• Shorter intervals can apply to equipment in poor condition, and some jurisdictions exempt certain small or low-pressure equipment entirely.\nVerify the interval, the inspecting authority and the posting requirement for the specific jurisdiction, and write in your report that these requirements vary and were confirmed locally.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the separated flue\nA gas-fired boiler's flue has separated at a joint four feet above the appliance, leaving a visible gap.\nAnswer: flue misaligned or disconnected. Combustion gases are entering an occupied building. Escalate before documenting, and ask whether the appliance should be shut down pending repair.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the boiler room as storage\nCardboard boxes 2 feet from the boiler, a can of paint thinner on a shelf above the burner, and a mop and bucket.\nAnswer: the boxes and the thinner are both inside the three-foot criterion; the thinner above the burner is the worse placement. The mop and bucket are not combustible storage in the relevant sense. Do note that a boiler room used as a janitorial closet is how the rest of it begins.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the certificate question\nA client's boiler certificate expired eight months ago and they ask whether it will fail their NSPIRE inspection.\nAnswer: the honest answer has two halves. Boiler certification is a state or local regime, so confirm the specific requirement with the authority having jurisdiction — and treat it as a live compliance and insurance obligation independent of NSPIRE. Do not tell them it does not matter, and do not tell them it is a scored deficiency, unless you have verified which it is for that jurisdiction and program.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the plant that cannot keep up\nIn January, with an outside temperature of 22 degrees, four units on the top floor of a building served by a central boiler measure 61 to 64 degrees with thermostats at maximum.\nAnswer: the HVAC criterion is not met in those units. Record the outside temperature, the indoor temperature in each unit, the thermostat setting, and the time — four numbers that turn an argument into evidence. The root cause is a plant, distribution or balancing problem, and it should be presented that way rather than as four unrelated unit findings.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the CO detector that is not there\nA boiler room with two gas-fired boilers adjacent to a community room has no carbon monoxide detection anywhere in the Inside area.\nAnswer: CO detection is required in the Inside area, statutory, no extension. Inexpensive to correct and severe exposure without it."},{"heading":"HVAC — the seasonal criterion and how to inspect it honestly","body":"THE STANDARD: HVAC\n• When the OUTSIDE temperature is BELOW 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT: the resident must be able to maintain a MINIMUM OF 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT inside through a safe heating source.\n• When the OUTSIDE temperature is 68 DEGREES FAHRENHEIT OR ABOVE: the air conditioning system or device must be OPERATIONAL.\n• A permanently installed heating source is required for climate zones HUD designates by notice.\n• UNVENTED SPACE HEATERS burning gas, oil or kerosene are PROHIBITED in units.\nSCORING NOTE: deferred from scoring until October 1, 2026 — cited and corrected now, deducting from that date.\n\nTHE HONEST-INSPECTION PROBLEM\nThe criterion is seasonal, which means half the year you cannot test half the standard. There is a professional way to handle this and an unprofessional one.\n\nTHE UNPROFESSIONAL WAY: run the furnace in August, feel warm air, and write \"heat operational.\"\nTHE PROFESSIONAL WAY: record the outside temperature and the date, state which half of the criterion was testable, test what you can, inspect the equipment for evident defects and safety conditions regardless of season, and schedule the untestable half for the appropriate season with a written recommendation.\n\nWHAT YOU CAN INSPECT IN ANY SEASON\n• The equipment exists, is permanently installed where required, and is not obviously damaged.\n• Combustion venting is intact — flue and chimney criteria apply year round.\n• No unvented gas, oil or kerosene space heaters are present.\n• Filters, accessible ductwork, and condensate handling.\n• Thermostats present and functional.\n• Combustible storage within three feet of the appliance.\n• Electrical connections and disconnects.\n• Evidence of a problem: soot, scorching, corrosion at the flue, water staining under an air handler, a condensate pan full of standing water.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — testing in August\nOutside temperature 84 degrees. The unit's central AC runs, the fan moves air, and the supply air is within a degree of the return air.\nAnswer: at 68 degrees or above the air conditioning system or device must be OPERATIONAL. A system that runs but does not cool is not operational in any useful sense. RECORD BOTH TEMPERATURES — a number turns an argument into evidence. Heating is not testable on this date; say so and schedule it.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — testing in January\nOutside temperature 34 degrees. A unit's thermostat is set to 75; after forty minutes the room measures 63 degrees.\nAnswer: the resident cannot maintain 68 degrees. Record the outside temperature, the setting, the measured temperature, and the elapsed time. Then look for the cause — an undersized or failed heat source, a distribution problem, or envelope loss such as a broken window or failed weatherstripping, which is its own separate finding.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the kerosene heater\nA living room has a kerosene space heater in use.\nAnswer: prohibited outright. Remove it, and investigate WHY it is there — a resident using a kerosene heater almost always has an underlying heating failure that is its own finding, and treating the symptom leaves the real deficiency in place.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the window unit as the only cooling\nA unit in a hot climate has no central AC; the only cooling is a resident-owned window unit, which is permanently installed in the bedroom's only egress window.\nAnswer: two separate issues. The blocked egress is a top-tier Unit finding and must be corrected. Whether the property is obligated to provide cooling depends on what the property was built with and on HUD's designated climate-zone requirements — confirm rather than assume. Record the facts precisely and flag the question rather than answering it from instinct.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the standing condensate\nAn air handler closet has a condensate pan holding about an inch of water with a secondary drain that has never been connected, and the ceiling below shows staining.\nAnswer: record the condensate condition and the water staining as evidence of intrusion, and check for mold-like substance measured against the 4-square-inch, 1-square-foot and 9-square-foot thresholds. This is a small mechanical failure that reliably becomes a Severe unit finding if left alone — exactly the kind of preventive catch that justifies a pre-inspection engagement."},{"heading":"Fire sprinkler systems","body":"THE STANDARD: SPRINKLER ASSEMBLY\nThe core recorded condition: a sprinkler head assembly ENCASED OR OBSTRUCTED by an item or object WITHIN 18 INCHES of the head.\n\nTHE EXCLUSIONS — know these cold, because they keep items off a punch list that were never going to be cited:\n• Heads that lack 18 inches of clearance because of a permanent feature of the BUILT ENVIRONMENT — a closet, wall-mounted kitchen cabinets, a permanently installed light fixture, an exit sign — are NOT deficiencies.\n• A PARKED VEHICLE within 18 inches of an assembly is NOT the deficiency.\n\nWHAT ELSE TO INSPECT\n• PAINTED HEADS. Overspray insulates the thermal element and delays or prevents activation. A painted head is replaced, not cleaned.\n• CORRODED OR DAMAGED HEADS, and bent or displaced deflectors.\n• MISSING HEADS and missing escutcheons.\n• ITEMS HUNG FROM HEADS OR PIPING. Coat hangers, wire, decorations. This risks accidental discharge and defeats the spray pattern, and it is endemic in storage and laundry rooms.\n• RISER AND CONTROL VALVES accessible, unobstructed, and in the correct position.\n• GAUGES reading within their normal range.\n• INSPECTION, TESTING AND MAINTENANCE RECORDS available. These belong in the certificate and service file from Chapter 8.\n\nJURISDICTION\nSprinkler inspection, testing and maintenance intervals are governed by adopted fire code and by national standards as adopted locally, and enforcement sits with the local authority having jurisdiction. Intervals for different activities — visual inspection, flow testing, internal inspection — differ from one another and by jurisdiction. Verify locally and say so in your report.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — reading the exclusions\nA storage room has boxes to within 10 inches of a head. A corridor has an exit sign 8 inches from a head. A carport has a parked car 14 inches below a head. A laundry room has a coat hanger looped over a head.\nAnswer: the boxes are a deficiency. The exit sign is excluded. The parked car is excluded. The coat hanger is a deficiency and a serious one. Four observations, two findings, and the difference is knowing the exclusions.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the painted head\nA basement corridor head has been overspray-painted during a repaint.\nAnswer: a damaged assembly. Replace, do not clean. Also flag the process issue — a painter who oversprayed one head almost certainly oversprayed others, so inspect the full extent of that repaint.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the storage-room ceiling\nA storage room's shelving reaches to within 8 inches of the ceiling across the whole room, with several heads above it.\nAnswer: systemic obstruction. Within the Inside area this collapses to one scored instance under the duplicate rule, but every head is obstructed and the correction is a real reorganization of the room. Recommend a marked clearance line painted on the wall at 18 inches below the heads — a permanent, visible control that survives staff turnover.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the closed valve\nA riser control valve is found in the partially closed position with no supervisory tamper device.\nAnswer: a partially closed control valve compromises the entire system. Escalate immediately to someone with authority; do not simply record it. Then request the inspection, testing and maintenance records.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the concealed head\nA resident has hung a decorative fabric panel from the ceiling in a unit, passing about 6 inches beneath a concealed sprinkler head.\nAnswer: an object obstructing within 18 inches, in the Unit area. Resident-installed does not exempt it, exactly as with blocked egress. Include it in the standard resident notice, since decorations near heads recur seasonally."},{"heading":"Fire extinguishers and fire alarm systems","body":"FIRE EXTINGUISHERS\nTHE RECORDED CONDITION: a service tag that is MISSING, ILLEGIBLE, or EXPIRED — where the date on the service tag has exceeded ONE YEAR.\nTHE EXCLUSION: if the property provides an invoice or report from the servicing fire extinguisher company, a missing-tag deficiency should NOT be recorded, provided the report date is NO MORE THAN ONE YEAR from the inspection date.\n\nALSO CHECK, on every extinguisher:\n• Present in its designated location and mounted.\n• Unobstructed and visible.\n• Gauge needle in the operable range.\n• Pin and tamper seal intact.\n• No visible damage, corrosion or discharge.\n\nFIRE ALARM SYSTEMS\nWhat to observe:\n• The control panel is accessible and not in a persistent TROUBLE or SILENCED condition. Photograph the panel display verbatim.\n• Devices — pull stations, horns, strobes, smoke and heat detectors — present, mounted, undamaged, and unobstructed.\n• Inspection, testing and maintenance records available.\n• Where a panel shows trouble, request the record and the service history rather than accepting a verbal explanation.\n\nSMOKE AND CO ALARMS IN COMMON AREAS\nThe Inside area must include at least one battery-operated or hard-wired smoke detector in proper working condition on EACH LEVEL of the property, and carbon monoxide detection per chapters 9 and 11 of the 2018 International Fire Code. Both requirements are statutory and were not part of any NSPIRE compliance extension.\n\nFIRE-LABELED DOORS\nIntact, self-closing and self-latching where required, with the label present and legible and no field modifications defeating the rating. Common defects: a failed or disconnected closer, a door wedged or hooked open, a latch that does not engage, a gap opened by frame movement, and holes drilled through the leaf for hardware or signage.\nSCORING NOTE: one of the six deferred-scoring affirmative requirements.\n\nJURISDICTION\nExtinguisher servicing, fire alarm testing intervals and fire-door inspection requirements are set by adopted fire code and enforced by the local authority having jurisdiction, and they vary. Confirm locally and state in your report that you did.\n\nWORKED CALL 1 — the tag versus the invoice\nExtinguisher A has a tag dated 14 months ago. Extinguisher B has no tag, and the manager produces a service company invoice dated four months ago covering all extinguishers.\nAnswer: A is a deficiency. B is not — the invoice within one year satisfies the exclusion. Two extinguishers, opposite answers, and the difference is a piece of paper. This is the clearest single argument for the certificate and service file being a product rather than overhead.\n\nWORKED CALL 2 — the discharged extinguisher\nAn extinguisher has a current tag but the gauge needle sits in the recharge zone and the tamper seal is broken.\nAnswer: a current tag does not make a discharged extinguisher functional. Record the gauge reading and the broken seal specifically. The tag criterion and the condition of the device are two different things.\n\nWORKED CALL 3 — the persistent trouble condition\nThe fire alarm panel shows an amber trouble light with the audible silenced; staff say it has been that way \"for a while.\"\nAnswer: record the panel display verbatim, photograph it, request the most recent inspection, testing and maintenance record, and escalate. A system in persistent trouble is not functioning as designed, and the absence of a service record is itself the client's problem.\n\nWORKED CALL 4 — the blocked pull station\nA corridor pull station is behind a stacked pallet of maintenance supplies.\nAnswer: an obstructed manual initiating device, and depending on the corridor width it may also be an exit access obstruction, which is a top-tier egress finding. Record both, distinctly. Free to correct.\n\nWORKED CALL 5 — the drilled fire door\nA fire-labeled stairwell door has two holes drilled through the leaf where a resident-installed sign was mounted, and the label has been painted over and is illegible.\nAnswer: field modification defeating the rating, plus an illegible label. Both are fire-door conditions in the deferred-scoring group. Explain the practical point to the client: a penetration through a rated leaf compromises the assembly, and the repair generally requires the door manufacturer's approved method rather than filling the holes with whatever is on the truck."},{"heading":"The certificate and service file — the deliverable for this unit","body":"Everything in this chapter converges on one artifact. Build it for every client and it does four jobs at once: it satisfies documentation obligations, it defeats specific citations, it supports technical review, and it is what the inspector asks for on site.\n\nWHAT GOES IN IT\n\nFIRE PROTECTION\n• Fire extinguisher service records — the servicing company's invoice or report, which is what defeats a missing-tag citation when dated within the past year.\n• Sprinkler inspection, testing and maintenance records, by activity and date.\n• Fire alarm inspection, testing and maintenance records, plus any outstanding trouble or deficiency notices from the service company.\n• Fire door inspection records where locally required.\n\nMECHANICAL\n• Boiler inspection certificates as required by the jurisdiction, with the interval and the issuing authority noted.\n• Water heater and HVAC service records.\n• Any pressure vessel certificates required locally.\n\nCONVEYANCE\n• Elevator operating certificate, most recent inspection report, maintenance contract, and callback history.\n\nENVIRONMENTAL\n• Pest control service records — which support both the condition history and a beyond-owner-control argument in a technical review.\n• Any remediation records for mold or water intrusion, including the source repair and not just the surface remediation.\n\nINSPECTION HISTORY\n• Prior NSPIRE or REAC reports, draft and final, with the sample size read off each one.\n• Annual self-inspection records for the required three-year retention.\n\nHOW TO ORGANIZE IT SO IT ACTUALLY GETS USED\n• One index page listing every document, its date, its expiration where applicable, and where the original lives.\n• A calendar of renewals with 60-day reminders. This is the part clients value most and the part they never build themselves.\n• A single physical or digital location that site staff can produce on demand, because an inspector standing in a lobby will not wait while someone searches an email archive.\n\nTHE THREE SENTENCES THAT SELL IT\n1. \"This is what defeats a citation — an extinguisher invoice inside a year is the difference between a finding and no finding.\"\n2. \"This is what a technical review runs on, and the window is 45 calendar days from the report. There is no time to build a file after the report arrives.\"\n3. \"This is what proves you met your annual self-inspection obligation when HUD asks for the records, which they can.\"\n\nSame file, three justifications, and at least one lands with any client. It is also, not incidentally, low-effort recurring work with high perceived value — which makes it one of the better products in your catalogue."}]}];
export const EXTRA_CHAPTER_QUIZZES = {"ch9":{"title":"Chapter 9 Quiz — Unit Deficiencies","questions":[{"q":"A kitchen outlet cover is chipped at one corner, leaving a measured 7/16-inch opening with no conductor visible. Correct call?","opts":["Exposed conductor deficiency","Not a deficiency — under the 1/2-inch criterion with no exposed conductor","Severe","Moderate"],"ans":1,"exp":"The criterion is an opening or gap greater than 1/2 inch. Recommend replacement anyway, but do not score it."},{"q":"A ceiling fixture is missing its bulb, leaving an open energized socket. Correct call?","opts":["Not a deficiency — a bulb is a consumable","Cite it — HUD lists a missing lightbulb among the examples of an exposed conductor","Cite it only in common areas","Cite it as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"A missing lightbulb leaving an open socket is a listed example under the Electrical — Conductor, Outlet, and Switch standard. Carry bulbs."},{"q":"A bathroom receptacle has no test or reset button, but the panel has a GFCI breaker serving the circuit and the receptacle trips when tested. Correct call?","opts":["Deficiency — the receptacle must be a GFCI device","Not a deficiency — protection may be provided at the breaker","Deficiency only if the bathroom has a tub","Deficiency, deferred from scoring"],"ans":1,"exp":"This is the most common false positive in the program. Always test rather than judging from the device's appearance."},{"q":"A bedroom has one working outlet, no ceiling fixture, and the resident's floor lamp plugged in. Compliant?","opts":["Yes — the room is lit","No — the requirement is two working outlets, or one outlet plus a PERMANENTLY INSTALLED light fixture; a lamp is furniture","Yes, if the lamp is provided by management","Only in units built before 1978"],"ans":1,"exp":"Permanently installed is the operative phrase, and it is worth being firm about."},{"q":"A water heater's TPR discharge pipe terminates 1 inch above the drain pan. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — lower is safer","Deficiency — the compliant window is 2 to 6 inches above the flood level","Compliant if the pan drains","Deficiency only if the valve leaks"],"ans":1,"exp":"Too low risks blockage and is outside the window, just as too high is."},{"q":"Which is NOT a listed water heater discharge piping deficiency?","opts":["Piping that is capped","Piping that slopes upward","Piping that terminates 4 inches above the floor drain","Piping made of unsuitable material"],"ans":2,"exp":"Four inches is inside the 2-to-6-inch compliant window. Capped, upward-sloping, damaged and unsuitable-material piping are all deficiencies."},{"q":"A kitchen sink cabinet floor is swollen and stained but nothing is dripping. Correct call?","opts":["No finding without an active drip","The evidence supports a plumbing leak finding, and any mold-like substance present is a separate finding under its own thresholds","Cosmetic only","Cite only the mold"],"ans":1,"exp":"Warped and stained surfaces are listed evidence. Two standards can be triggered by one cabinet."},{"q":"An interior stair has four risers and no handrail. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — handrails are required at five or more risers","Deficiency — a handrail is required where there are four or more risers","Compliant if the stair is under 30 inches total rise","Deferred from scoring"],"ans":1,"exp":"Four or more risers triggers the handrail requirement. Count them; three versus four is the entire call."},{"q":"An interior landing sits 34 inches above the floor below with a 27-inch guardrail. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant","Deficiency — a guardrail is required at 30 inches or more above grade and must be at least 30 inches in height; this one is undersized","Deficiency only if the rail is loose","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"Both criteria are 30 inches. Note that guardrail is in the six deferred-scoring categories — cited and corrected now, deducting from October 1, 2026."},{"q":"Outside temperature is 84 degrees. What can you conclude about the unit's heating?","opts":["It passes by default","It fails by default","Nothing testable — record that heating was not testable on this date, inspect for evident defects, and schedule a cold-season check","Test it anyway and record the result"],"ans":2,"exp":"The heating criterion applies when the outside temperature is below 68 degrees. Saying 'not testable, scheduled' is professional; guessing is not."},{"q":"A resident is using a kerosene space heater in the living room. Correct call?","opts":["Permitted if the resident owns it","Prohibited — unvented space heaters burning gas, oil or kerosene are not allowed in units — and investigate why it is there","Permitted in units without central heat","Cite it as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"Prohibited outright, and its presence usually signals an underlying heating failure that is its own finding."},{"q":"A bathroom fan runs and moves air but discharges into the attic rather than to the exterior. Best professional response?","opts":["Ignore it — the fan works","Describe it precisely, evaluate it against the ventilation criterion, and flag it in writing as a root cause of future mold findings","Cite it as Life-Threatening","Cite it under the mold standard"],"ans":1,"exp":"The purpose of the standard is for moisture to escape. Even where the citation is uncertain, the written root-cause recommendation is exactly the value a client cannot get from maintenance staff."},{"q":"A bathroom ceiling shows mold-like discoloration of 30 by 36 inches plus a patch of 9 by 8 inches. Severity?","opts":["Below de minimis","Moderate","Severe — 8.0 square feet cumulative falls in the 1-to-9-square-foot band","Life-Threatening"],"ans":2,"exp":"1,080 plus 72 square inches equals 1,152, or 8.0 square feet. Measure and add rather than eyeballing."},{"q":"A 2019-built unit has a correctly placed, working smoke alarm with a sealed 10-year battery. Compliant?","opts":["No — hardwired is always required","Yes — the hardwired-only requirement applies to construction or substantial rehabilitation after December 2022","No — 10-year batteries are not accepted","Only if it is interconnected"],"ans":1,"exp":"Get the construction or substantial-rehab date before making this call; it changes the answer."},{"q":"In the recommended unit walk order, what comes first?","opts":["The kitchen appliances","The entry door, then every window, then every alarm","The electrical panel","Environmental conditions"],"ans":1,"exp":"Doors, windows and alarms first — they are the highest-severity findings and the easiest to miss if you leave them to the end of a long walk."}]},"ch10":{"title":"Chapter 10 Quiz — Inside (Common Area) Deficiencies","questions":[{"q":"Why should a pre-inspection engagement start with Inside areas?","opts":["They carry the highest weights","No scheduling friction, high-severity low-cost findings, weights only about 9 percent below Unit, and everything is under management's control","They are the only areas REAC inspects fully","Because units are inspected by a different inspector"],"ans":1,"exp":"Inside runs about 91 percent of Unit weight, needs no resident coordination, and concentrates cheap high-severity findings."},{"q":"Five stairwell fire doors are propped open across the property. For scoring:","opts":["Five Inside deductions","One scored Inside instance — but all five must still be corrected, and clearing four buys nothing in score terms","One deduction per building","They are averaged"],"ans":1,"exp":"The duplicate rule collapses within the Inside area. Say all three parts of that to the client, not just the convenient one."},{"q":"An electrical room panel is missing two breakers, leaving open ports with the bus visible. At a sample of 22, what is the score effect and the repair cost?","opts":["2.48 points, under ten dollars in filler plates","1.65 points, several hundred dollars","0.10 points, free","No deduction — panels are not inspected"],"ans":0,"exp":"54.5 divided by 22 equals 2.48 points. Dollar for dollar this is among the highest-return findings on any property."},{"q":"A corridor emergency light shows a lit green power indicator but does not illuminate when the test button is pressed. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — the indicator is lit","Deficient — the indicator confirms power, not battery function; a unit that fails the test does not work in an outage","Compliant if the corridor has windows","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"Test every one, and record which you tested. Documented testing puts a client in a materially better position."},{"q":"A rear exit door has a chain and padlock on the inside, removed each morning per staff. Correct response?","opts":["Not a finding since it is removed daily","A lock requiring a key from the egress side on an exit door — escalate immediately to someone with authority rather than logging it","Cite it as Moderate","Note it as advisory"],"ans":1,"exp":"It was in place at the time of observation, and the daily-removal explanation is not a defense. This is an escalate-now finding."},{"q":"A stairwell fire door is wedged open. Beyond removing the wedge, what should the recommendation address?","opts":["Nothing else","Why the wedge exists — heat, airflow or a delivery route — because otherwise it returns before the next inspection","Replacing the door","Removing the closer"],"ans":1,"exp":"Naming the cause is what distinguishes a consultant's punch list from a maintenance to-do list."},{"q":"A fire extinguisher has no tag, but the manager produces a servicing company invoice dated four months ago. Correct call?","opts":["Cite it — the tag is missing","Do not cite it — a report or invoice dated no more than one year from the inspection satisfies the exclusion","Cite it at reduced severity","Cite it only if the gauge is low"],"ans":1,"exp":"The exclusion is explicit, and it is a clean example of documentation defeating a citation."},{"q":"A fire extinguisher tag is dated 14 months ago. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — the tag is present and legible","Deficient — the tag date has exceeded one year","Compliant if the gauge reads full","Deficient only in Public Housing"],"ans":1,"exp":"Missing, illegible, or exceeding one year are all tag deficiencies."},{"q":"Which is a sprinkler deficiency?","opts":["An exit sign 8 inches from a head","A wall-mounted cabinet 12 inches from a head","A coat hanger looped over a head","A parked vehicle 14 inches below a head"],"ans":2,"exp":"Permanent built-environment features and parked vehicles are excluded. Anything hanging from a head risks accidental discharge and defeats the spray pattern."},{"q":"A basement corridor sprinkler head was overspray-painted during a repaint. Correct response?","opts":["Clean the paint off","Cite it as a damaged assembly and have the head replaced — paint insulates the thermal element","Ignore it if the head is otherwise intact","Cite it as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"Painted heads are replaced, not cleaned, because the thermal response is compromised."},{"q":"A boiler room contains boxes 2 feet from the boiler and paint thinner on a shelf above the burner. Correct call?","opts":["Neither is citable in a locked mechanical room","Both are combustible storage within three feet of a heat appliance; the thinner above the burner is the more serious placement","Only the thinner","Only the boxes"],"ans":1,"exp":"Three feet is the criterion for both, and correction cost is zero."},{"q":"A gas boiler's flue pipe has separated at a joint. What is the right sequence of action?","opts":["Photograph it and continue the walk","Escalate immediately to someone with authority and ask whether the appliance should be shut down, then document it","Note it for the final report","Refer it to the fire department"],"ans":1,"exp":"Combustion gases are entering an occupied building. Escalation comes before documentation."},{"q":"A boiler room with two gas-fired boilers has no carbon monoxide detection anywhere in the Inside area. Correct call?","opts":["Not required in common areas","Required — CO detection applies to the Inside area as well as units, and the requirement is statutory with no extension","Required only after October 1, 2026","Required only if residents access the room"],"ans":1,"exp":"The final rule requires CO detection in both the Inside area and the Unit, and it was never part of any NSPIRE extension."},{"q":"A manager asks whether they must install a call-for-aid system to pass NSPIRE. Correct answer?","opts":["Yes, in all elderly properties","No — call-for-aid systems are not required under the inspection protocol; if one exists it should function","Yes, in all buildings over three stories","Only in Public Housing"],"ans":1,"exp":"Not required. Telling a client confidently that they do not need to spend money is one of the fastest ways to earn trust."},{"q":"A common-area water heater's TPR discharge terminates 14 inches above the floor drain. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — the criterion applies only in units","Deficient — the 2-to-6-inch window applies to common-area equipment too","Compliant if the drain is unobstructed","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"Same standard, different area. People skip common-area equipment because they associate the criterion with dwelling units."}]},"ch11":{"title":"Chapter 11 Quiz — Outside Deficiencies","questions":[{"q":"Roughly what fraction of the Unit weight does an Outside deficiency carry at the same severity?","opts":["About 25 percent","About 50 percent","About 83 percent","About 95 percent"],"ans":2,"exp":"49.6 versus 60.0 at Life-Threatening, 33.1 versus 40.0 at Severe. Outside is cheap because the repairs are cheap, not because the weights are trivial."},{"q":"Three walkway locations measure a 5/8-inch lift, a 7/8-inch lift, and a 2-1/2-inch open joint. How many trip hazard deficiencies?","opts":["Three","Two — the 7/8-inch vertical and the 2-1/2-inch horizontal","One","None"],"ans":1,"exp":"Vertical criterion is 3/4 inch, horizontal is 2 inches. The 5/8-inch lift is under the threshold."},{"q":"A second-floor exterior walkway 11 feet above grade has a guardrail measuring 29 inches. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant","Deficient — undersized against the 30-inch minimum height; measure at several points since deck slope often makes rails compliant at one end only","Compliant if the rail is secure","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"Both the trigger and the minimum height are 30 inches. Guardrail sits in the six deferred-scoring categories."},{"q":"A chain-link fence has a cut top rail with a protruding spike at chest height beside a walkway. A concrete curb elsewhere has a chipped, rough edge. Which meets the sharp-edge criterion?","opts":["Both","Neither","The spike only — the test is a cut or puncture likely to require emergency care","The curb only"],"ans":2,"exp":"Roughness is not the test; the plausible severity of the injury is."},{"q":"A detached downspout is discharging onto a roof valley, with staining on the wall below and ceiling staining in the top-floor unit. How should this be presented?","opts":["As two unrelated findings","As one Outside drainage repair that is the root of Unit-area conditions, with any mold measured against its thresholds","As a Unit finding only","As cosmetic"],"ans":1,"exp":"Envelope failures are where Outside genuinely moves a score, because one repair retires a cluster of highest-weighted Unit findings."},{"q":"A wall pack at a building entry has a cracked housing with a weather-exposed wiring splice visible. Correct call?","opts":["A lighting deficiency","An exposed conductor at Outside weight of 49.6 — not a lighting nuisance","Cosmetic","Not inspectable from grade"],"ans":1,"exp":"The exposed conductor is the finding, and at 49.6 it is not far below what the same condition costs in a unit."},{"q":"Why should a pre-inspection include an after-dark site visit?","opts":["Inspectors work at night","Because fixtures that look intact in daylight may not illuminate, and there is no reliable way to tell otherwise","To check resident behavior","It is required by the standard"],"ans":1,"exp":"Photograph fixtures by day, confirm illumination after dark. It routinely produces findings that daylight cannot."},{"q":"A fire escape's drop ladder is padlocked and a resident's storage bin sits on the landing. Correct call?","opts":["Not citable — fire escapes are rarely used","Two egress obstructions on the same assembly, both top-tier, both free to correct","One Moderate finding","A security finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"Obstructions accumulate on fire escapes precisely because nobody uses them, which is why they are so often overlooked."},{"q":"A four-foot retaining wall above a resident walkway shows a horizontal crack and about an inch of outward bulge. Best response?","opts":["Log it for the report","Escalate immediately, recommend closing the path pending evaluation, and recommend an engineer rather than classifying the repair yourself","Cite it as Moderate and move on","Recommend repointing"],"ans":1,"exp":"Structural failure above a path of travel. Knowing the limit of your own competence is part of the professional standard."},{"q":"Which of these is the largest category of pre-inspection spending that buys no points?","opts":["Grinding sidewalk lifts","Landscaping and mulch","Replacing pole lamps","Repairing exterior stair treads"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unless overgrowth obstructs a path, blocks a fixture or grows into the envelope, landscaping is not a scored condition."},{"q":"What is the correct nuance when telling a client that appearance is not scored?","opts":["Appearance is worthless and should be ignored","Appearance is not SCORED, but it matters for residents, lease-up, and the tone an inspector arrives in — the distinction is what you are paid to draw","Appearance is scored under the Outside area","Appearance only matters at small properties"],"ans":1,"exp":"Overstating the case costs you credibility. The precise claim is that it is not scored."},{"q":"A trash enclosure has a broken gate, overflowing containers, and rodent droppings and burrows. What should you note about the pest evidence?","opts":["It is not citable outdoors","It is an Outside-area infestation finding — and if pest evidence also appears in units and common areas, the extent escalation moves the condition to the top tier property-wide","It is only citable if live rodents are seen","It is a Low finding"],"ans":1,"exp":"Evidence alone suffices, and extent across multiple units and common areas is the escalation trigger."},{"q":"An exterior stair to a basement entry has five risers and no handrail; another to a first-floor entry has three risers and no handrail. How many deficiencies?","opts":["Two","One — the five-riser stair","None","One — the three-riser stair"],"ans":1,"exp":"Four or more risers triggers the handrail requirement. Count them."},{"q":"Playground equipment is unsafe and cannot be repaired promptly. What is the correct interim step?","opts":["Leave it and note it","Secure or remove the equipment, documented as an interim measure with a scheduled permanent repair","Post a warning sign only","Nothing until the report is issued"],"ans":1,"exp":"Interim measures must be recorded as interim, with the full repair scheduled — the same discipline as any other interim correction."},{"q":"Ranked by points recovered per dollar, what belongs at the top of an Outside punch list?","opts":["Sealcoating the parking lot","Any exposed conductor at an exterior fixture, meter base, disconnect or junction","Repainting the project sign","Pressure washing walkways"],"ans":1,"exp":"Top of the severity ladder at 49.6, and the correction is usually a cover or a housing."}]},"ch12":{"title":"Chapter 12 Quiz — Elevator Standards & Certificates","questions":[{"q":"An elevator's operating certificate is expired. Under NSPIRE, what is recorded?","opts":["A Severe deficiency","A Moderate deficiency","No deficiency — the certificate is verified during the inspection but neither its absence nor its expiration is itself a recorded deficiency","The elevator is taken out of the inspection"],"ans":2,"exp":"This is the rule most consultants get wrong. The consequence of an expired certificate arrives through state and local law, insurance and liability — not through the NSPIRE score."},{"q":"An inspector records a deficiency for a missing elevator certificate. What is your move?","opts":["Accept it and correct the certificate","File a technical review on the criteria-not-met basis under the inspector-error ground","File under conditions beyond the owner's control","Nothing can be done"],"ans":1,"exp":"A clean, checkable criteria-not-met filing — and the kind of detail that pays for a consulting fee on its own."},{"q":"What is the measurable criterion for elevator mis-leveling?","opts":["More than 1/4 inch","More than 1/2 inch","More than 3/4 inch between the car and the adjoining floor","More than 1 inch"],"ans":2,"exp":"There should not be a 3/4-inch or greater difference between the elevator car and the adjoining floor."},{"q":"A five-stop elevator measures: lobby flush, 2nd floor 1/2 inch low, 3rd floor 7/8 inch high, 4th floor 1-1/8 inch low, 5th floor flush. How many deficiencies?","opts":["One","Two — the 3rd and 4th floors","Three","None"],"ans":1,"exp":"Only the 3rd and 4th floors exceed the 3/4-inch criterion. Record every landing's measurement anyway — the pattern helps the contractor and protects the client."},{"q":"Which of these is a listed elevator deficiency?","opts":["An expired certificate","The safety edge device has malfunctioned or is inoperable","A scuffed cab wall","A machine room without a floor drain"],"ans":1,"exp":"Inoperable elevator, doors not fully opening and closing, cab not level beyond 3/4 inch, and a malfunctioning or inoperable safety edge device are the listed conditions."},{"q":"How should you test the safety edge device?","opts":["Press it with your hand","Obstruct the closing doors with a soft object such as a rolled towel and confirm the doors reverse — at every landing","Assume it works if the doors close","Ask the maintenance staff"],"ans":1,"exp":"Never use a hand or foot. Test at every landing, and record which device — mechanical edge or electronic detector — you tested."},{"q":"Staff report the elevator intermittently stops between floors, but it operates normally during your visit. Correct response?","opts":["Cite it as inoperable","You cannot cite what you did not observe — record the reported history, request the maintenance log and last inspection report, and recommend diagnosis in writing before the inspection window","Ignore it","Cite it as Moderate"],"ans":1,"exp":"Honest and useful: no manufactured citation, but the client gets the information and the recommendation."},{"q":"An elevator pit has two inches of standing water with the pit light a foot above it and corroded buffer bases. What is the correct handling?","opts":["Note it for the report","Escalate immediately, record the depth, corrosion and proximity of electrical components, and determine the water's source — a pit pumped without fixing the source refills","Recommend pumping only","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"Standing water around energized pit equipment is an escalate-now condition, not a report line item."},{"q":"An elevator machine room contains paint cans, broken chairs and boxes. Correct call?","opts":["Not citable — machine rooms are not resident areas","Unrelated storage in a machine room; assess combustibles against the three-foot criterion relative to any heat source and record access obstruction separately","Cite as Low","Cite only if the room is unlocked"],"ans":1,"exp":"Zero-cost correction that recurs at nearly every property."},{"q":"Which statement about elevator inspection intervals is accurate?","opts":["NSPIRE sets a uniform national interval","Intervals, the inspecting authority, posting requirements and periodic testing requirements are set by state and local authority and vary by jurisdiction","All jurisdictions require annual inspection","Intervals are set by the maintenance contractor"],"ans":1,"exp":"Assume nothing and verify with the local authority having jurisdiction — and say so in writing in your report."},{"q":"Which technical review ground is most likely to arise around elevators, boilers, sprinklers and fire alarms?","opts":["Conditions beyond the owner's control","Modernization work in progress","Conflict with state or local code","Fast correction"],"ans":2,"exp":"These are the systems where local jurisdictions impose their own inspection, permitting and certificate regimes. The filing needs the code citation and ideally a letter from the authority having jurisdiction."},{"q":"What belongs in the elevator maintenance file even though the certificate is not scored?","opts":["Only the certificate","Current certificate with a 60-day renewal reminder, the most recent inspection report, the current maintenance contract, recent service and callback records, and local periodic test records","Only the maintenance contract","Nothing — NSPIRE does not require a file"],"ans":1,"exp":"A pattern of callbacks for the same fault is the best predictor of a failure during an inspection window."},{"q":"In the recommended report structure, where does an expired elevator certificate belong?","opts":["Section 1, NSPIRE-scored findings","Section 2, not NSPIRE-scored but required — with the local compliance obligation stated","Section 3, reliability observations","It should be omitted"],"ans":1,"exp":"Separating scored findings from real-but-unscored obligations is what lets a client allocate money and urgency correctly."},{"q":"Which finding should be escalated to an elevator contractor immediately rather than logged?","opts":["A scuffed cab floor covering","A safety edge device that does not reverse the doors","A faded floor indicator","A certificate expiring next month"],"ans":1,"exp":"Along with pit water near energized equipment, mis-leveling over the criterion, reports of the car stopping between floors, and an inoperable emergency communication device."},{"q":"Why should you never write 'elevator OK' in a report?","opts":["It is prohibited","Because a report that shows the method — what you tested, at which landings, and what you measured — is worth several times one that shows only the conclusion","Because elevators always have deficiencies","Because it invites a technical review"],"ans":1,"exp":"Method is what makes the report defensible and what the client is actually paying for."}]},"ch13":{"title":"Chapter 13 Quiz — Boiler, HVAC & Fire System Standards","questions":[{"q":"What is the HVAC criterion when the outside temperature is below 68 degrees Fahrenheit?","opts":["The heating system must run","The resident must be able to maintain a minimum of 68 degrees inside through a safe heating source","The unit must measure at least 65 degrees","The thermostat must be set to 72"],"ans":1,"exp":"And when the outside temperature is 68 or above, the air conditioning system or device must be operational."},{"q":"You inspect in August at 84 degrees outside. What is the professional way to handle the heating criterion?","opts":["Run the furnace and record 'heat operational'","Record the outside temperature and date, state which half of the criterion was testable, inspect the equipment for evident defects regardless of season, and schedule a cold-season check in writing","Mark it as failed","Skip HVAC entirely"],"ans":1,"exp":"The criterion is seasonal. Documenting what was and was not testable is what distinguishes a professional report."},{"q":"A unit's AC runs and moves air, but supply air is within a degree of return air at 84 degrees outside. Correct call?","opts":["Operational — the system runs","Not operational in any useful sense — record BOTH temperatures, since a number turns an argument into evidence","Not testable","Deficient only if the resident complains"],"ans":1,"exp":"Running is not the same as operational. Recording the supply and return temperatures is what makes the finding defensible."},{"q":"Which is prohibited in a unit?","opts":["A window air conditioner","An unvented space heater burning gas, oil or kerosene","A programmable thermostat","A portable fan"],"ans":1,"exp":"Prohibited outright — and its presence usually signals an underlying heating failure that is its own separate finding."},{"q":"A gas boiler's flue has separated at a joint. What comes first?","opts":["Photograph and continue the walk","Escalate to someone with authority and ask whether the appliance should be shut down, then document","Note it in the final report","Recommend annual servicing"],"ans":1,"exp":"Combustion gases are entering an occupied building. Escalation precedes documentation."},{"q":"Boiler certificate inspection intervals are set by:","opts":["The NSPIRE standards","REAC","State or local law, and they vary — a common pattern is annual for high-pressure power boilers and biennial for low-pressure heating boilers, but the jurisdiction governs","The maintenance contractor"],"ans":2,"exp":"Verify the interval, the inspecting authority and the posting requirement locally, and say in your report that you did."},{"q":"In January at 22 degrees outside, four top-floor units measure 61 to 64 degrees with thermostats at maximum. What should you record?","opts":["That the heat is inadequate","The outside temperature, the indoor temperature in each unit, the thermostat setting and the time — and present it as one plant, distribution or balancing problem rather than four unrelated findings","Only the lowest reading","Nothing until the boiler is serviced"],"ans":1,"exp":"Four numbers turn an argument into evidence, and the root-cause framing is what the client cannot produce themselves."},{"q":"Which sprinkler observation IS a deficiency?","opts":["An exit sign 8 inches from a head","A wall-mounted kitchen cabinet 12 inches from a head","A parked vehicle 14 inches below a head","Boxes stacked to within 10 inches of a head"],"ans":3,"exp":"Permanent built-environment features and parked vehicles are excluded. Stored material is exactly what the 18-inch rule targets."},{"q":"A resident hangs a fabric panel 6 inches beneath a concealed sprinkler head in their unit. Correct call?","opts":["Not citable — resident property","An object obstructing within 18 inches, in the Unit area; resident installation does not exempt it","Advisory only","Citable only in common areas"],"ans":1,"exp":"Same principle as blocked egress — the obligation runs to the owner. Put it in the standard resident notice, since decorations near heads recur seasonally."},{"q":"A storage room's shelving reaches to within 8 inches of the ceiling across the whole room, under several heads. Best recommendation?","opts":["Replace the heads","Reorganize the room and paint a marked clearance line on the wall 18 inches below the heads — a permanent visible control that survives staff turnover","Remove the shelving entirely","Nothing — one scored instance is trivial"],"ans":1,"exp":"The score collapses to one instance, but every head is obstructed. A physical control is what actually keeps it corrected."},{"q":"An extinguisher has a current tag but the gauge is in the recharge zone and the tamper seal is broken. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — the tag is current","Deficient — record the gauge reading and broken seal; the tag criterion and the condition of the device are two different things","Compliant if the extinguisher is mounted","Advisory only"],"ans":1,"exp":"A current tag does not make a discharged extinguisher functional."},{"q":"A fire alarm panel has shown an amber trouble light with the audible silenced for weeks. What should you do?","opts":["Accept the staff explanation and move on","Record the display verbatim, photograph it, request the most recent inspection, testing and maintenance record, and escalate","Reset the panel","Cite it as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"A system in persistent trouble is not functioning as designed, and the absence of a service record is itself the client's problem."},{"q":"A fire-labeled stairwell door has two drilled holes from a resident sign and a painted-over illegible label. Correct call?","opts":["Cosmetic only","Field modification defeating the rating plus an illegible label — both fire-door conditions, in the deferred-scoring group, and the repair generally requires the manufacturer's approved method","Fill the holes with any available filler","Not citable if the door still closes"],"ans":1,"exp":"A penetration through a rated leaf compromises the assembly; a field patch does not restore the rating."},{"q":"Which document defeats a missing fire extinguisher tag citation?","opts":["A photograph of the extinguisher","A servicing company invoice or report dated no more than one year from the inspection","A staff log entry","A copy of the fire code"],"ans":1,"exp":"The exclusion is explicit — and it is the clearest single argument for the certificate and service file being a product rather than overhead."},{"q":"What is the most valued element of a certificate and service file, and the one clients never build themselves?","opts":["The index page","A calendar of renewals with 60-day reminders","Copies of the standards","Prior inspection photographs"],"ans":1,"exp":"The renewal calendar is what turns a folder of documents into an operating control."}]}};
export const EXTRA_UNIT_TESTS = {"u4":{"title":"Unit 4 Cumulative Test — Complete Deficiency Standards","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"The trip hazard criteria are:","opts":["1/2 inch vertical, 1 inch horizontal","3/4 inch vertical, 2 inches horizontal","1 inch vertical, 3 inches horizontal","Any visible differential"],"ans":1,"exp":"Measure it. A 1/2-inch lift is not a deficiency."},{"q":"A handrail is required on a stair with:","opts":["Two or more risers","Three or more risers","Four or more risers","Six or more risers"],"ans":2,"exp":"Count risers rather than estimating — three versus four is the whole call."},{"q":"A guardrail is required where a walking surface is at least how far above grade, and what is the minimum height?","opts":["24 inches above grade, 36 inches high","30 inches above grade, 30 inches high","36 inches above grade, 42 inches high","42 inches above grade, 30 inches high"],"ans":1,"exp":"Both figures are 30 inches. Guardrail is in the deferred-scoring group until October 1, 2026."},{"q":"A bedroom has one working outlet and a working ceiling fixture. Compliant on the minimum electrical and lighting requirement?","opts":["No — two outlets are always required","Yes — one working outlet plus a permanently installed light fixture satisfies it","Only if the fixture is a ceiling fan","Only in units built after 2022"],"ans":1,"exp":"Two outlets, OR one outlet plus a permanent fixture."},{"q":"A bathroom has no fan, no passive vent, and a window painted shut. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — a window exists","Moderate deficiency — a painted-shut window is not operable","Severe","Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"NSPIRE accepts an exhaust fan, a passive vent, or an operable window; none is available here."},{"q":"A bathroom fan runs audibly but moves no air. Correct call?","opts":["Compliant — it is present and running","Deficient — present but not operable does not satisfy the requirement","Advisory only","Compliant if a window exists"],"ans":1,"exp":"The tissue test demonstrates it."},{"q":"A sharp edge is a deficiency when it can cause:","opts":["Any scratch","A cut or puncture hazard likely to require emergency care, such as stitches","Damage to clothing","Any discomfort"],"ans":1,"exp":"The severity of the plausible injury is the test, not the roughness of the edge."},{"q":"Which is an Outside component?","opts":["An interior stairwell handrail","A boiler room","A retaining wall","An elevator machine room"],"ans":2,"exp":"Retaining walls, fencing, walkways, signs and detached garages are all Outside."},{"q":"Why start a pre-inspection engagement in the Inside areas?","opts":["They carry the highest weights","No scheduling friction, cheap high-severity findings, weights only about 9 percent below Unit, and everything under management's control","Units are inspected separately","It is required"],"ans":1,"exp":"The highest yield of correctable points per hour is almost always in mechanical rooms, stairwells and corridors."},{"q":"An extinguisher has no tag but the manager produces a servicing company invoice dated four months ago. Correct call?","opts":["Cite it","Do not cite it — an invoice or report dated within one year satisfies the exclusion","Cite at reduced severity","Cite only if the gauge is low"],"ans":1,"exp":"The clearest example of documentation defeating a citation."},{"q":"Roughly what share of the Unit weight does an Outside deficiency carry at the same severity?","opts":["25 percent","50 percent","83 percent","95 percent"],"ans":2,"exp":"Outside is cheap because the repairs are cheap, not because the weights are trivial."},{"q":"Which is the largest category of pre-inspection spending that buys no points?","opts":["Grinding sidewalk lifts","Landscaping and mulch","Replacing pole lamps","Repairing exterior stair treads"],"ans":1,"exp":"Unless overgrowth obstructs a path, blocks a fixture or grows into the envelope, it is not a scored condition."},{"q":"A wall pack at a building entry has a cracked housing with a visible wiring splice. Correct call?","opts":["A lighting deficiency","An exposed conductor at Outside weight of 49.6","Cosmetic","Not inspectable"],"ans":1,"exp":"The exposed conductor is the finding, and 49.6 is not far below the in-unit weight."},{"q":"A vacant unit shows mouse droppings and gnaw marks but no live rodents. Citable?","opts":["No — live pests are required","Yes — evidence alone is sufficient","Only if a resident reports it","Only in common areas"],"ans":1,"exp":"Advise closing entry points, not just cleaning, or the finding returns next year."},{"q":"In the recommended unit walk order, what comes first?","opts":["Kitchen appliances","The entry door, then every window, then every alarm","The electrical panel","Environmental conditions"],"ans":1,"exp":"The highest-severity findings, and the easiest to miss at the end of a long walk."}]},"u5":{"title":"Unit 5 Cumulative Test — Building Systems & Certificates","passingScore":70,"questions":[{"q":"An elevator's operating certificate is expired. Under NSPIRE, what is recorded?","opts":["A Severe deficiency","A Moderate deficiency","No deficiency — the certificate is verified but neither its absence nor its expiration is itself a recorded deficiency","The elevator is excluded from the inspection"],"ans":2,"exp":"The consequence arrives through state and local law, insurance and liability — not the NSPIRE score."},{"q":"The elevator mis-leveling criterion is a difference of more than:","opts":["1/4 inch","1/2 inch","3/4 inch","1 inch"],"ans":2,"exp":"Between the elevator car and the adjoining floor. Measure at every landing."},{"q":"How should the safety edge device be tested?","opts":["With a hand","By obstructing the closing doors with a soft object at every landing and confirming they reverse","By assuming it works if the doors close","By asking maintenance"],"ans":1,"exp":"Never use a hand or foot, and record which device you tested."},{"q":"Boiler inspection certificate intervals are set by:","opts":["The NSPIRE standards","REAC","State or local law, and they vary by jurisdiction and equipment type","The insurance carrier alone"],"ans":2,"exp":"A common pattern is annual for high-pressure power boilers and biennial for low-pressure heating boilers, but the jurisdiction governs."},{"q":"The HVAC criterion when the outside temperature is below 68 degrees Fahrenheit is that:","opts":["The heating system must run","The resident must be able to maintain a minimum of 68 degrees inside through a safe heating source","The unit must reach 65 degrees","The thermostat must be set to 72"],"ans":1,"exp":"And at 68 or above outside, the air conditioning system or device must be operational."},{"q":"Which is prohibited in a unit?","opts":["A window air conditioner","An unvented space heater burning gas, oil or kerosene","A programmable thermostat","A portable fan"],"ans":1,"exp":"Prohibited outright — and its presence usually signals an underlying heating failure."},{"q":"You inspect in August at 84 degrees outside. The professional handling of the heating criterion is:","opts":["Run the furnace and record 'heat operational'","Record the date and outside temperature, state which half of the criterion was testable, inspect the equipment for evident defects, and schedule a cold-season check in writing","Mark it failed","Skip HVAC"],"ans":1,"exp":"Documenting what was and was not testable is what distinguishes a professional report."},{"q":"A sprinkler head assembly is deficient when obstructed by an item within:","opts":["6 inches","12 inches","18 inches","24 inches"],"ans":2,"exp":"With exclusions for permanent built-environment features and parked vehicles."},{"q":"A basement sprinkler head has been overspray-painted. Correct response?","opts":["Clean the paint off","Cite it as a damaged assembly and replace the head — paint insulates the thermal element","Ignore it","Cite as Low"],"ans":1,"exp":"Replaced, not cleaned — and inspect the full extent of the repaint."},{"q":"Call-for-aid systems under the NSPIRE inspection protocol are:","opts":["Required in all elderly properties","Not required — but if a property has one, it should function","Required in buildings over three stories","Required only in Public Housing"],"ans":1,"exp":"Telling a client confidently that they do not need to spend money is one of the fastest ways to earn trust."}]}};
